{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JkGAxvuVHtKdYraKgZ56t6","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Reviewer Guidelines - Summary\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, here's what is available regarding reviewer information:\n\n## Review Criteria for Regular Papers\n\nPapers are evaluated on:\n- \"Significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- Degree of objective establishment and potential scientific/technological impact\n- Relevance to robotic learning\n\nThe conference welcomes submissions combining experimental and theoretical findings.\n\n## Desk Rejection Criteria\n\nPapers may be desk rejected for:\n1. **Formatting issues** — incorrect length or format\n2. **Anonymity violations** — author identification in manuscript or materials\n3. **Scope issues** — insufficient connection to robot learning through either:\n   - Explicit learning questions for physical robots, OR\n   - Testing solutions on physical robots\n\n## Reviewer Qualifications\n\nReviewers should possess:\n- Expertise in \"robotics, robot learning, machine learning and complementary fields\"\n- Minimum of 3 first-author publications in major robotics venues (CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, IJRR, TRO, RAL)\n- Publications in core machine learning conferences\n\n## Notable Limitation\n\n**The provided content does not include:** a detailed review form, specific numerical rating scales, scoring rubrics, or individual evaluation dimensions with numerical values. These elements are not present in the webpage excerpt provided."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"7197a65b-f3a5-4629-aefb-1a30e6a87154","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:05:33.379Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":133733,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Reviewer Guidelines - Summary\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, here's what is available regarding reviewer information:\n\n## Review Criteria for Regular Papers\n\nPapers are evaluated on:\n- \"Significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- Degree of objective establishment and potential scientific/technological impact\n- Relevance to robotic learning\n\nThe conference welcomes submissions combining experimental and theoretical findings.\n\n## Desk Rejection Criteria\n\nPapers may be desk rejected for:\n1. **Formatting issues** — incorrect length or format\n2. **Anonymity violations** — author identification in manuscript or materials\n3. **Scope issues** — insufficient connection to robot learning through either:\n   - Explicit learning questions for physical robots, OR\n   - Testing solutions on physical robots\n\n## Reviewer Qualifications\n\nReviewers should possess:\n- Expertise in \"robotics, robot learning, machine learning and complementary fields\"\n- Minimum of 3 first-author publications in major robotics venues (CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, IJRR, TRO, RAL)\n- Publications in core machine learning conferences\n\n## Notable Limitation\n\n**The provided content does not include:** a detailed review form, specific numerical rating scales, scoring rubrics, or individual evaluation dimensions with numerical values. These elements are not present in the webpage excerpt provided.","durationMs":4313,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TxcHoS2HDVXQT7AQ2NS6WY","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Review Form Fields\n\nThe guidelines specify these main review components:\n\n1. **Summary of Paper**: Reviewers should \"re-express the paper's position clearly and fairly\" in their own words rather than copying from abstracts.\n\n2. **Main Review**: Evaluation across six dimensions\n\n## Evaluation Criteria/Dimensions\n\nThe document outlines these assessment areas:\n\n- **Originality**: Whether problems/methods are novel, how work differs from prior contributions, adequacy of related work citations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness, claim support through analysis/experiments, methodological appropriateness, completeness\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality, organization, informativeness, reproducibility of results\n- **Significance**: Importance of results, likelihood of adoption, advancement of state-of-the-art\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning, application of learning-based methods, evaluation context (simulation/robots), audience interest\n- **Limitations**: Appreciation for honestly reported limitations; emphasis on identifying unreported shortcomings\n\n## Numerical Scale\n\n**The document does not provide a specific numerical rating scale or scoring rubric.** It references evaluation dimensions but omits concrete point values, score ranges, or quantitative thresholds for acceptance decisions.\n\n## Key Reviewer Expectations\n\nReviewers should be \"constructive,\" \"concrete,\" and \"courteous,\" avoid common mistakes (unsupported claims, demanding specific methodologies), and recognize that novel insights merit consideration even without state-of-the-art performance."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"d8737a8d-d788-445a-9521-1d5378071ec0","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:05:35.295Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Review Form Fields\n\nThe guidelines specify these main review components:\n\n1. **Summary of Paper**: Reviewers should \"re-express the paper's position clearly and fairly\" in their own words rather than copying from abstracts.\n\n2. **Main Review**: Evaluation across six dimensions\n\n## Evaluation Criteria/Dimensions\n\nThe document outlines these assessment areas:\n\n- **Originality**: Whether problems/methods are novel, how work differs from prior contributions, adequacy of related work citations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness, claim support through analysis/experiments, methodological appropriateness, completeness\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality, organization, informativeness, reproducibility of results\n- **Significance**: Importance of results, likelihood of adoption, advancement of state-of-the-art\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning, application of learning-based methods, evaluation context (simulation/robots), audience interest\n- **Limitations**: Appreciation for honestly reported limitations; emphasis on identifying unreported shortcomings\n\n## Numerical Scale\n\n**The document does not provide a specific numerical rating scale or scoring rubric.** It references evaluation dimensions but omits concrete point values, score ranges, or quantitative thresholds for acceptance decisions.\n\n## Key Reviewer Expectations\n\nReviewers should be \"constructive,\" \"concrete,\" and \"courteous,\" avoid common mistakes (unsupported claims, demanding specific methodologies), and recognize that novel insights merit consideration even without state-of-the-art performance.","durationMs":5813,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
