{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bx2iTuMz59ACWipTGDoBdS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough info on CoRL review standards. Let me also check the CoRL 2024 statistics for more details on the rating scale.\n\nFrom the data:\n- CoRL 2024 used a scale that appears to be 1-4 (mean 3.01)\n- CoRL 2023 used a scale of 1-10 (mean 6.52)\n- CoRL 2025 acceptance rate: ~84% (221/263) — but that seems very high, probably it's the submission/desk reject numbers are different\n\n\n- The 84% acceptance rate is suspiciously high, so let me verify against CoRL 2024 stats to get a clearer picture of their actual acceptance standards.\n\nBased on what I've gathered, CoRL evaluates submissions across dimensions like originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance, and limitations, with rating scales that have varied between 1-4 and 1-10 across different years.\n\nI'll go with the 1-10 scale from CoRL 2023 since it's the most recent well-documented standard and aligns with typical ML venue practices. Now I'm ready to dispatch the two reviewers in parallel—R1 will focus on methods and experiments while R2 covers novelty and related work—then run through the roundtable discussion process.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_read_input_tokens":56985,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"fbf6c2da-c42a-4a75-94ee-29afbc83364f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bx2iTuMz59ACWipTGDoBdS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have the paper, review context, and CoRL guidelines. Dispatching R1 (Methods+Experiments) and R2 (Novelty+Related Work) in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_read_input_tokens":56985,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"5ad8a686-9b83-4ed7-a2f1-c2bfeb9928dd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bx2iTuMz59ACWipTGDoBdS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F9MWD8xUBVCyK2j19WEhKH","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning)\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below)\n- Acceptance rate historically ~25-35%\n- CoRL values: robotics relevance, empirical rigor, real-world applicability, honest limitations\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: An inference-time module for flow-matching VLAs that extracts per-DOF conformal statistics from K=10 samples (CGS: 45D features with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage) and feeds them into a lightweight CRM (131K params, ~12ms overhead) for dimension-selective action correction. Uses Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) driven by a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) signal (self-referential, no external supervision).\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. Per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver (12pp ablation gap over generic residual)\n2. COC provides effective self-referential signal for ACI (ρ=0.61, 2.5x faster convergence)\n3. Orthogonal to training-time methods (stacked on SRPO → 88.7% LIBERO-Plus SOTA)\n\n**Main results (Table 2)**:\n- Base SmolVLA: 97.3% LIBERO / 57.8% LIBERO-Plus\n- ACR-VLA: 96.5% LIBERO / 72.8% LIBERO-Plus (+15.0pp, 12.4ms overhead)\n- SRPO: 98.1% LIBERO / 82.1% LIBERO-Plus\n- ACR-VLA+SRPO: 97.8% LIBERO / 88.7% LIBERO-Plus (+6.6pp)\n\n**Ablations (Table 3)**:\n- A1 (no ACI, static calib): 68.2% → ACI contributes 4.6pp\n- A2 (no CRM, sample selection only): 63.5% → CRM learned correction worth 9.3pp over selection\n- A3 (CRM w/o conformal features): 60.8% → conformal features contribute 12.0pp\n- A4 (CRM w/ scalar uncertainty): 66.8% → per-DOF > scalar by 6.0pp\n\n**Additional experiments**:\n- Per-perturbation breakdown (Table 4): gains on 6/7 types (camera +19.5, noise +17.8, robot-state +16.2, lighting +13.4, background +11.8, layout +5.2; language +2.1 not significant)\n- COC validation (Table 5): ρ=0.61, p=3e-5; ACI coverage 0.89 vs static 0.78; 28% volume narrowing\n- A5 (CGS vs Bonferroni): joint Mahalanobis beats Bonferroni by 3.4pp, Bonferroni 35% larger volumes\n- A6 (COC vs episode-level ACI): similar final perf (72.8 vs 72.1), COC 2.5x faster convergence, +4.2pp early\n- A7 (epistemic vs aleatoric): CGS 45D (72.8%) >> DiU 7D (63.9%), combined 52D only +0.7pp more\n- Feature importance: conformal radius 48%, std 31%, ellipsoid volume 21%; perturbation-specific top features differ\n- SnapFlow: 71.2% at 38ms (4.9x speedup), 87% diversity retained\n- MVP point-reaching: per-DOF 0.0735 < scalar 0.0788 < none 0.0882\n\n**Experiment design context** (from review_context.yaml — the full experiment plan):\nThe paper covers all 14 planned experiments. The context lists expected results that match what the paper reports. Planned tables/figures include:\n- Tables 1-8 plus S1-S5 (supplementary)\n- Figures 1-14 plus S1-S5\nHowever, the 7-page paper only shows Tables 1-8 inline; the supplementary figures/tables are referenced but appear to be in supplementary material not included in this PDF.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences, your own words)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methodological soundness and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps, experimental concerns, missing controls)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues** (presentation, notation, etc.)\n6. **Scores**: Rate each dimension 1-10:\n   - Soundness (methodological rigor)\n   - Empirical quality (experimental coverage, statistical rigor)\n   - Clarity\n   - Significance\n   - Overall recommendation\n   - Confidence (1-5, 5=expert)\n\nBe critical but fair. Flag issues with statistical methodology, missing baselines, potential confounds, and experimental gaps. Check whether the ablations truly isolate the claimed contributions. Consider whether K=10 is well-justified, whether the CRM training procedure introduces confounds, and whether LIBERO-Plus alone is sufficient for the claims made."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_read_input_tokens":56985,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"deae2aec-8ebf-4629-9391-9f2ac5a16deb"}
