{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgmxUcc4n1J1vVYc21Ph9p","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n10\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction\\\\via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models}\n15\t\n16\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n17\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t%% ============================================================\n24\t%% ABSTRACT\n25\t%% ============================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tFlow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models achieve near-saturated performance on standard manipulation benchmarks yet suffer catastrophic robustness failures under distribution shift.\n28\tWe present \\textbf{ACR-VLA} (Adaptive Conformal Refinement for VLAs), an inference-time module that extracts per-degree-of-freedom (per-DOF) conformal statistics from multiple flow-matching samples and feeds them into a lightweight Conformal Refinement Module (CRM) to perform \\emph{dimension-selective} action correction.\n29\tThree components constitute the pipeline: (i)~\\textbf{Conformal Geometry Statistics (CGS)}, which computes 45-dimensional structured uncertainty features encoding per-DOF standard deviations, conformal radii, and ellipsoid volumes from $K{=}10$ samples with Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage; (ii)~\\textbf{Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI)} driven by a novel \\textbf{Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC)} signal that maintains calibrated coverage without external supervision; and (iii)~a \\textbf{CRM} (131K parameters, ${\\sim}12$\\,ms overhead) that outputs a learned residual correction conditioned on the CGS features.\n30\tOn LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves a base SmolVLA from 57.8\\% to 72.8\\% success rate (+15.0\\,pp) while preserving nominal LIBERO performance (96.5\\% vs.\\ 97.3\\%).\n31\tWhen stacked on SRPO---the current training-time state of the art at 82.1\\%---ACR-VLA achieves \\textbf{88.7\\%}, establishing a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA and demonstrating that inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal gains to training-time methods.\n32\tAblations confirm that per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver (12.0\\,pp gap over generic residual), and that the COC self-referential signal enables 2.5$\\times$ faster ACI convergence than episode-level supervision.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t%% ============================================================\n36\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n37\t%% ============================================================\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\n40\tFlow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have achieved remarkable success on standard robotic manipulation benchmarks, with top systems reaching 97--99\\% success rates on LIBERO~\\cite{liu2024libero}.\n41\tHowever, LIBERO-Plus~\\cite{liberoplus2025} reveals a sobering reality: these same models collapse to 50--60\\% success under realistic distribution shifts including camera viewpoint changes, sensor noise, and lighting variations.\n42\t\n43\tExisting approaches to VLA robustness fall into two categories.\n44\t\\emph{Training-time methods} such as SRPO~\\cite{srpo2025}, RobustVLA, and DiG-Flow improve the base policy through data augmentation or reinforcement learning, achieving up to 82.1\\% on LIBERO-Plus.\n45\t\\emph{Inference-time methods} such as ReconVLA~\\cite{reconvla2025} and SAFE~\\cite{safe2025} use uncertainty estimates for sample selection or anomaly detection, but rely on scalar uncertainty scores that discard the rich per-DOF covariance structure available from flow-matching samples.\n46\t\n47\tWe identify a critical gap: no existing method leverages \\emph{structured, per-DOF conformal statistics} as input features for a learned correction module.\n48\tReconVLA uses scalar Euclidean distance for sample selection---it cannot synthesize an action better than any individual sample.\n49\tA2C2-style generic residual policies lack calibrated uncertainty conditioning.\n50\tCP-in-the-Loop employs conformal prediction for planning but not as features for learned correction.\n51\t\n52\t\\textbf{ACR-VLA} bridges this gap with three contributions:\n53\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Per-DOF conformal features for dimension-selective correction.}\n55\t  We show that 45-dimensional CGS features (per-DOF std, conformal radius, and ellipsoid volume) enable a lightweight CRM to selectively correct high-uncertainty DOFs while preserving confident predictions. Ablations demonstrate a 12.0\\,pp gap over CRM without conformal features and a 6.0\\,pp gap over scalar uncertainty conditioning.\n56\t\\item \\textbf{COC as a self-referential ACI signal.}\n57\t  Chunk Overlap Consistency---a measure of inter-chunk prediction agreement---correlates with task success ($\\rho{=}0.61$, $p{<}10^{-4}$) and enables ACI convergence in 47 episodes vs.\\ 120 for episode-level supervision, without requiring external reward signals.\n58\t\\item \\textbf{Orthogonal gains to training-time methods.}\n59\t  ACR-VLA stacked on SRPO yields 88.7\\% on LIBERO-Plus (+6.6\\,pp over SRPO alone), demonstrating that inference-time conformal correction and training-time RL optimization address complementary failure modes.\n60\t\\end{enumerate}\n61\t\n62\t%% ============================================================\n63\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n64\t%% ============================================================\n65\t\\section{Related Work}\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Robustness of VLA models.}\n68\tLIBERO-Plus exposes the fragility of VLAs under distribution shift.\n69\tTraining-time approaches include domain randomization~(RobustVLA), flow-matching augmentation~(DiG-Flow), and RL fine-tuning~(SRPO, 82.1\\% LIBERO-Plus SOTA).\n70\tInference-time methods include ReconVLA (scalar uncertainty-based sample selection) and SAFE (anomaly detection for safe fallback).\n71\tACR-VLA is the first to combine multivariate conformal prediction with learned residual correction at inference time.\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Conformal prediction in robotics.}\n74\tConformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees.\n75\tRecent work applies CP to robot planning (CP-in-the-Loop), policy gating (UPS), and safe set construction.\n76\tThese methods treat CP outputs as binary decisions (inside/outside the conformal set) rather than as continuous features for downstream learning.\n77\tACR-VLA instead feeds per-DOF conformal statistics directly into a learned correction module, enabling graded, dimension-selective responses.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty quantification for action correction.}\n80\tDiU estimates aleatoric uncertainty via a single forward pass; A2C2 learns generic residual corrections.\n81\tNeither provides calibrated, per-DOF epistemic uncertainty features.\n82\tACR-VLA's CGS captures epistemic uncertainty (elevated under distribution shift, low in-distribution), which we show is fundamentally more informative for action correction than aleatoric estimates (Section~\\ref{sec:a7}).\n83\t\n84\t%% ============================================================\n85\t%% 3. METHOD\n86\t%% ============================================================\n87\t\\section{Method}\n88\t\n89\tACR-VLA operates as a post-hoc inference-time module on top of any flow-matching VLA.\n90\tGiven a base VLA $\\pi_\\theta$ that generates action chunks via flow matching, ACR-VLA draws $K{=}10$ samples, computes structured uncertainty features, and produces a corrected action.\n91\tThe pipeline consists of three components: CGS (\\S\\ref{sec:cgs}), ACI+COC (\\S\\ref{sec:aci}), and CRM (\\S\\ref{sec:crm}).\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Conformal Geometry Statistics (CGS)}\n94\t\\label{sec:cgs}\n95\t\n96\tGiven $K$ action samples $\\{a^{(k)}\\}_{k=1}^K$ from the base VLA, where each $a^{(k)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ ($d{=}7$ DOFs for a typical manipulator: 3 translation + 3 rotation + 1 gripper), CGS computes:\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Sample statistics.}\n99\tThe sample mean $\\bar{a} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_k a^{(k)}$ and covariance $\\hat{\\Sigma} = \\text{LW}(\\{a^{(k)}\\})$, where LW denotes Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage.\n100\tShrinkage is essential: at $K{=}10$ and $d{=}7$, the ratio $p/n = 0.7$ makes the raw sample covariance ill-conditioned.\n101\tLedoit--Wolf reduces the condition number from ${\\sim}10^4$ to ${\\sim}10^2$.\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Per-DOF features.}\n104\tFor each DOF $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$, CGS extracts: (i) marginal standard deviation $\\sigma_j = \\sqrt{\\hat{\\Sigma}_{jj}}$; (ii) conformal radius $r_j$, defined as the $j$-th marginal projection of the Mahalanobis ellipsoid at the $(1{-}\\alpha)$ quantile; and (iii) a shared ellipsoid volume $V = \\det(\\hat{\\Sigma})^{1/2}$.\n105\tThis yields a 15-dimensional feature vector per DOF (for a 7-DOF action chunk spanning 2--3 timesteps) plus the scalar volume, totaling 45 dimensions.\n106\t\n107\t\\paragraph{Nonconformity score.}\n108\tThe joint Mahalanobis distance $s(a) = (a - \\bar{a})^\\top \\hat{\\Sigma}^{-1} (a - \\bar{a})$ serves as the nonconformity score for conformal calibration.\n109\tThis captures cross-dimensional correlations that per-DOF Bonferroni correction misses (Section~\\ref{sec:a5}).\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Adaptive Conformal Inference with COC}\n112\t\\label{sec:aci}\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC).}\n115\tFlow-matching VLAs predict action chunks spanning multiple future timesteps.\n116\tConsecutive predictions overlap in their temporal coverage.\n117\tCOC measures the agreement between overlapping portions of adjacent chunks:\n118\t\\begin{equation}\n119\t\\text{COC}_t = 1 - \\frac{\\|\\hat{a}_{t}^{\\text{overlap}} - \\hat{a}_{t-1}^{\\text{overlap}}\\|}{\\|\\hat{a}_{t-1}^{\\text{overlap}}\\| + \\epsilon}\n120\t\\end{equation}\n121\tHigh COC ($>0.85$) indicates stable, self-consistent predictions; low COC ($0.4$--$0.6$) signals policy disagreement across timesteps.\n122\tCOC correlates with task success (Spearman $\\rho{=}0.61$, $p{=}3{\\times}10^{-5}$; Table~\\ref{tab:coc}).\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{ACI update rule.}\n125\tACI maintains a running conformal threshold $\\hat{q}_t$ and updates it based on whether the COC signal indicates coverage:\n126\t\\begin{equation}\n127\t\\hat{q}_{t+1} = \\hat{q}_t + \\gamma(\\alpha - \\mathbf{1}[\\text{COC}_t < \\hat{q}_t])\n128\t\\end{equation}\n129\twhere $\\gamma$ is the step size and $\\alpha$ is the target miscoverage rate (0.1).\n130\tThis achieves long-run coverage guarantees without external task success labels.\n131\tACI converges in a median of 85 steps (47 episodes), narrowing ellipsoid volume by 28\\% compared to static calibration while maintaining 0.89 effective coverage (vs.\\ 0.78 for static; Table~\\ref{tab:aci}).\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Conformal Refinement Module (CRM)}\n134\t\\label{sec:crm}\n135\t\n136\tThe CRM is a lightweight MLP (131K parameters) that takes as input the concatenation of: (i)~the base VLA's observation embedding, (ii)~the 45D CGS feature vector, and (iii)~the mean action $\\bar{a}$, and outputs a residual correction $\\delta a$:\n137\t\\begin{equation}\n138\ta^* = \\bar{a} + \\text{CRM}(z_{\\text{obs}}, \\phi_{\\text{CGS}}, \\bar{a})\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\twhere $z_{\\text{obs}}$ is the frozen observation embedding from the base VLA and $\\phi_{\\text{CGS}}$ is the 45D CGS feature.\n141\t\n142\t\\paragraph{Training.}\n143\tCRM is trained on LIBERO demonstration data augmented with synthetic perturbations (camera shift, sensor noise, robot-state perturbation, lighting, background---5 augmentation types).\n144\tThe training objective minimizes L2 loss between the corrected action and the ground-truth demonstration action, with the CGS features computed from $K{=}10$ forward passes of the frozen base VLA on each augmented observation.\n145\t\n146\t\\paragraph{Inference overhead.}\n147\t$K{=}10$ sampling + CGS computation + CRM forward pass adds ${\\sim}12.4$\\,ms to inference, negligible for typical robot control loops at 10--20\\,Hz.\n148\t\n149\t%% ============================================================\n150\t%% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n151\t%% ============================================================\n152\t\\section{Experiments}\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Setup}\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Base VLA.} SmolVLA, a flow-matching VLA with competitive LIBERO performance (97.3\\% nominal).\n157\t\n158\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} (i)~LIBERO (nominal): 4 task suites, standard conditions. (ii)~LIBERO-Plus: the same tasks under 7 perturbation types (camera, sensor noise, robot state, lighting, background, layout, language) at levels L3--L5.\n159\t\n160\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Base SmolVLA (57.8\\% LIBERO-Plus), SRPO-finetuned SmolVLA (82.1\\% LIBERO-Plus SOTA).\n161\t\n162\t\\paragraph{ACR-VLA configuration.} $K{=}10$, Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage, ACI target coverage $1{-}\\alpha{=}0.9$, CRM 131K parameters.\n163\t\n164\t\\subsection{MVP Point-Reaching Validation}\n165\t\n166\tBefore deploying the full pipeline, we validate the core hypothesis---per-DOF conformal features outperform scalar alternatives---in a controlled 3D point-reaching environment (Table~\\ref{tab:mvp}).\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[h]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\caption{MVP point-reaching: MSE comparison across conditioning types ($K{=}10$, Ledoit--Wolf).}\n171\t\\label{tab:mvp}\n172\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n173\t\\toprule\n174\t\\textbf{Conditioning} & \\textbf{Total MSE} & \\textbf{vs.\\ None} & \\textbf{vs.\\ Scalar} \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tNone & 0.0882 & --- & --- \\\\\n177\tScalar & 0.0788 & $-10.7\\%$ & --- \\\\\n178\tPer-DOF & 0.0735 & $-16.7\\%$ & $-6.7\\%$ \\\\\n179\t\\bottomrule\n180\t\\end{tabular}\n181\t\\end{table}\n182\t\n183\tPer-DOF conditioning achieves 16.7\\% MSE reduction over no conditioning and 6.7\\% over scalar conditioning.\n184\tThe advantage is most pronounced on the high-noise X dimension, confirming that per-DOF features enable dimension-selective correction.\n185\tOracle-to-estimated covariance degradation at $K{=}10$ is ${\\sim}15$\\,pp, but the per-DOF $>$ scalar $>$ none ranking is preserved.\n186\t\n187\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus success rates (\\%). $\\Delta$ denotes absolute gain over the respective base model.}\n192\t\\label{tab:main}\n193\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n194\t\\toprule\n195\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{LIBERO} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Plus} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\textbf{Plus} & \\textbf{Overhead} \\\\\n196\t\\midrule\n197\tBase SmolVLA & 97.3 & 57.8 & --- & --- \\\\\n198\tACR-VLA (ours) & 96.5 & 72.8 & +15.0 & 12.4\\,ms \\\\\n199\t\\midrule\n200\tSRPO & 98.1 & 82.1 & --- & --- \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9MWD8xUBVCyK2j19WEhKH","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"ae0b8619-8681-46ce-b120-fd7685729d28","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:07:03.867Z"}
