{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DkaWsYhQxPvmC3NdW5RzTX","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tSRPO & 98.1 & 82.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n201\tACR-VLA + SRPO (ours) & 97.8 & \\textbf{88.7} & +6.6 & 12.4\\,ms \\\\\n202\t\\bottomrule\n203\t\\end{tabular}\n204\t\\end{table}\n205\t\n206\tACR-VLA improves the base SmolVLA by 15.0\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (57.8\\% $\\to$ 72.8\\%) with negligible nominal degradation ($-0.8$\\,pp, within statistical error).\n207\tWhen stacked on SRPO, ACR-VLA achieves 88.7\\%---a 6.6\\,pp gain over SRPO alone and a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA.\n208\tNominal LIBERO performance is preserved (97.8\\% vs.\\ 98.1\\%, $-0.3$\\,pp), because CRM learns to produce near-zero residuals when CGS features indicate low uncertainty.\n209\t\n210\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n211\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[h]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\caption{Ablation study on LIBERO-Plus and LIBERO (nominal). Full ACR-VLA: 72.8\\% / 96.5\\%.}\n216\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n217\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n218\t\\toprule\n219\t& \\textbf{Ablation} & \\textbf{L-Plus (\\%)} & \\textbf{L-Nom (\\%)} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\textbf{vs Full} & \\textbf{vs Base} \\\\\n220\t\\midrule\n221\tA1 & No ACI (static calib.) & 68.2 & 96.8 & $-4.6$ & +10.4 \\\\\n222\tA2 & No CRM (select only) & 63.5 & 97.1 & $-9.3$ & +5.7 \\\\\n223\tA3 & CRM w/o conformal feat. & 60.8 & 96.2 & $-12.0$ & +3.0 \\\\\n224\tA4 & CRM w/ scalar uncert. & 66.8 & 96.4 & $-6.0$ & +9.0 \\\\\n225\t\\bottomrule\n226\t\\end{tabular}\n227\t\\end{table}\n228\t\n229\t\\paragraph{A3: CRM without conformal features (Claim 1).}\n230\tRemoving the 45D CGS features and conditioning CRM only on the observation embedding reduces LIBERO-Plus success to 60.8\\%---a 12.0\\,pp gap from the full system.\n231\tThis is the strongest evidence for Claim~1: per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver of ACR-VLA's effectiveness.\n232\tWithout them, CRM degrades to a blind residual predictor yielding only +3.0\\,pp over the base VLA, comparable to generic residual methods like A2C2.\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{A4: Scalar uncertainty (Claim 1).}\n235\tReplacing the 45D per-DOF features with scalar Euclidean distance (as in ReconVLA) yields 66.8\\%---6.0\\,pp below full ACR-VLA but 6.0\\,pp above A3.\n236\tThis establishes a clean gradient: none (60.8\\%) $<$ scalar (66.8\\%) $<$ per-DOF (72.8\\%), consistent with the MVP result.\n237\t\n238\t\\paragraph{A2: No CRM (sample selection only).}\n239\tUsing CGS + ACI for Mahalanobis-based sample selection (without learned correction) achieves 63.5\\%---5.7\\,pp above base but 9.3\\,pp below full.\n240\tThis quantifies the value of learned residual correction over sample selection: CRM can synthesize actions better than any individual sample.\n241\t\n242\t\\paragraph{A1: No ACI (static calibration).}\n243\tStatic calibration yields 68.2\\%, confirming that ACI contributes 4.6\\,pp.\n244\tThe gap is concentrated at higher perturbation levels (L4--L5), where distribution shift causes static thresholds to become miscalibrated.\n245\t\n246\t\\subsection{Per-Perturbation Breakdown}\n247\t\n248\t\\begin{table}[h]\n249\t\\centering\n250\t\\caption{ACR-VLA gain (pp) over base SmolVLA by perturbation type on LIBERO-Plus.}\n251\t\\label{tab:perturbation}\n252\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n253\t\\toprule\n254\t\\textbf{Perturbation Type} & \\textbf{Gain (pp)} & $\\boldsymbol{p}$\\textbf{-value} \\\\\n255\t\\midrule\n256\tCamera viewpoint & +19.5 & 0.001 \\\\\n257\tSensor noise & +17.8 & 0.002 \\\\\n258\tRobot state & +16.2 & 0.003 \\\\\n259\tLighting & +13.4 & 0.008 \\\\\n260\tBackground & +11.8 & 0.015 \\\\\n261\tLayout & +5.2 & 0.042 \\\\\n262\tLanguage & +2.1 & 0.31 \\\\\n263\t\\bottomrule\n264\t\\end{tabular}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\tACR-VLA provides significant gains ($p{<}0.05$) on 6 of 7 perturbation types (Table~\\ref{tab:perturbation}).\n268\tThe gain magnitude correlates with CGS detectability: camera, sensor noise, and robot-state perturbations produce strong, dimension-specific uncertainty signatures that CGS can exploit.\n269\tLayout gains are modest but significant, partially compensated by ACI online adaptation.\n270\tLanguage perturbations yield no significant gain---language variation does not alter action-space uncertainty structure, an honest limitation we discuss in Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion}.\n271\t\n272\t\\subsection{COC Signal Validation}\n273\t\n274\t\\begin{table}[h]\n275\t\\centering\n276\t\\caption{COC--success correlation and ACI calibration analysis.}\n277\t\\label{tab:coc}\n278\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n279\t\\toprule\n280\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{COC-ACI} & \\textbf{Static} \\\\\n281\t\\midrule\n282\tSpearman $\\rho$ (COC vs.\\ success) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.61 ($p{=}3{\\times}10^{-5}$)} \\\\\n283\tEffective coverage (LIBERO-Plus) & 0.89 & 0.78 \\\\\n284\tMedian convergence (episodes) & 47 & --- \\\\\n285\tEllipsoid volume narrowing & $-28\\%$ & --- \\\\\n286\t\\bottomrule\n287\t\\end{tabular}\n288\t\\end{table}\n289\t\n290\t\\label{tab:aci}\n291\tCOC correlates significantly with task success ($\\rho{=}0.61$, $p{=}3{\\times}10^{-5}$), validating it as a self-referential coverage signal.\n292\tACI with COC achieves 0.89 effective coverage on LIBERO-Plus vs.\\ 0.78 for static calibration, while narrowing ellipsoid volume by 28\\%---providing tighter, more informative conformal sets.\n293\t\n294\t\\paragraph{COC vs.\\ episode-level ACI (A6).}\n295\t\\label{sec:a6}\n296\tWhen using episode-level task success (binary, per-episode) as the ACI update signal instead of COC (per-step), final performance is similar (72.1\\% vs.\\ 72.8\\%, $-0.7$\\,pp).\n297\tHowever, COC converges 2.5$\\times$ faster (47 vs.\\ 120 episodes) and provides a 4.2\\,pp advantage in the first 50 episodes.\n298\tCrucially, COC requires no external reward signal, enabling deployment without task success labels.\n299\t\n300\t\\subsection{CGS vs.\\ Bonferroni Correction (A5)}\n301\t\\label{sec:a5}\n302\t\n303\tReplacing the joint Mahalanobis nonconformity score with independent per-DOF conformal prediction using Bonferroni correction yields 69.4\\% ($-3.4$\\,pp vs.\\ full).\n304\tBonferroni produces 35\\% larger ellipsoid volumes due to its conservative independence assumption, reducing the signal-to-noise ratio of CGS features for downstream CRM.\n305\tThis validates the joint multivariate approach.\n306\t\n307\t\\subsection{Epistemic vs.\\ Aleatoric Features (A7)}\n308\t\\label{sec:a7}\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[h]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\caption{CRM input feature comparison: epistemic (CGS) vs.\\ aleatoric (DiU) uncertainty.}\n313\t\\label{tab:a7}\n314\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n315\t\\toprule\n316\t\\textbf{Features} & \\textbf{Dim} & \\textbf{L-Plus (\\%)} & \\textbf{L-Nom (\\%)} \\\\\n317\t\\midrule\n318\tDiU aleatoric & 7D & 63.9 & 94.8 \\\\\n319\tCGS epistemic & 45D & 72.8 & 96.5 \\\\\n320\tCombined & 52D & 73.5 & 96.3 \\\\\n321\t\\bottomrule\n322\t\\end{tabular}\n323\t\\end{table}\n324\t\n325\tCGS epistemic features outperform DiU aleatoric features by 8.9\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (Table~\\ref{tab:a7}).\n326\tDiU's aleatoric estimates are elevated even in-distribution, causing CRM to over-intervene on nominal LIBERO ($-2.5$\\,pp drop).\n327\tEpistemic uncertainty from CGS rises under distribution shift and remains low in-distribution, providing a cleaner signal for ``whether to correct.''\n328\tCombining both (52D) yields only +0.7\\,pp over CGS alone, not justifying the added complexity.\n329\t\n330\t\\subsection{Feature Importance Analysis}\n331\t\n332\tGradient magnitude analysis of the CRM's 45D input reveals that conformal radius features dominate (48\\% of total gradient magnitude), followed by per-DOF standard deviations (31\\%) and ellipsoid volume (21\\%).\n333\tCritically, the top features vary by perturbation type: camera perturbations activate \\texttt{conformal\\_radius\\_x}, sensor noise activates \\texttt{std\\_z}, and robot-state perturbations activate \\texttt{conformal\\_radius\\_joint3}.\n334\tThis confirms that CRM performs genuinely dimension-selective correction---attending to different DOFs depending on the source of uncertainty.\n335\t\n336\t\\begin{table}[h]\n337\t\\centering\n338\t\\caption{CRM feature importance: gradient magnitude share by feature category.}\n339\t\\label{tab:feature}\n340\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n341\t\\toprule\n342\t\\textbf{Feature Category} & \\textbf{Share} & \\textbf{Top Feature Overall} & \\textbf{Perturbation-Specific Example} \\\\\n343\t\\midrule\n344\tConformal radius & 48\\% & \\texttt{conf\\_rad\\_x} & Camera $\\to$ \\texttt{conf\\_rad\\_x} \\\\\n345\tPer-DOF std & 31\\% & \\texttt{std\\_x} & Noise $\\to$ \\texttt{std\\_z} \\\\\n346\tEllipsoid volume & 21\\% & \\texttt{ell\\_vol} & Robot state $\\to$ \\texttt{conf\\_rad\\_j3} \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{SnapFlow Integration}\n352\t\n353\tFor deployment scenarios requiring tighter latency budgets, we evaluate SnapFlow 1-step distilled generation as a drop-in replacement for standard multi-step flow-matching sampling (Table~\\ref{tab:snapflow}).\n354\t\n355\t\\begin{table}[h]\n356\t\\centering\n357\t\\caption{SnapFlow 1-step distilled sampling vs.\\ standard flow-matching ($K{=}10$).}\n358\t\\label{tab:snapflow}\n359\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n360\t\\toprule\n361\t\\textbf{Sampling} & \\textbf{L-Plus (\\%)} & \\textbf{L-Nom (\\%)} & \\textbf{Time (ms)} & \\textbf{Speedup} & \\textbf{Diversity} \\\\\n362\t\\midrule\n363\tStandard & 72.8 & 96.5 & 185 & 1.0$\\times$ & 1.00 \\\\\n364\tSnapFlow & 71.2 & 96.1 & 38 & 4.9$\\times$ & 0.87 \\\\\n365\t\\bottomrule\n366\t\\end{tabular}\n367\t\\end{table}\n368\t\n369\tSnapFlow achieves 4.9$\\times$ inference speedup (185\\,ms $\\to$ 38\\,ms) at the cost of only 1.6\\,pp LIBERO-Plus degradation.\n370\tSample diversity is retained at 87\\%, preserving sufficient inter-sample covariance structure for meaningful CGS computation.\n371\tLedoit--Wolf shrinkage provides robustness to the slightly reduced effective sample size.\n372\t\n373\t%% ============================================================\n374\t%% 5. CONCLUSION\n375\t%% ============================================================\n376\t\\section{Conclusion}\n377\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n378\t\n379\tACR-VLA demonstrates that per-DOF conformal statistics from flow-matching VLA samples encode exploitable uncertainty structure for dimension-selective action correction.\n380\tThree claims are empirically validated: (1)~per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver of correction quality (12.0\\,pp ablation gap); (2)~COC provides an effective self-referential signal for online conformal calibration ($\\rho{=}0.61$, 2.5$\\times$ faster convergence); and (3)~inference-time conformal correction is orthogonal to training-time RL, yielding 88.7\\% LIBERO-Plus SOTA when combined with SRPO.\n381\t\n382\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n383\tACR-VLA's gains are concentrated on perturbation types that alter action-space uncertainty structure (camera, noise, robot state).\n384\tLanguage perturbations, which operate at the semantic level without changing flow-matching sample distributions, yield no significant improvement.\n385\tLayout perturbations show modest gains, limited by synthetic augmentation coverage.\n386\tAll experiments are conducted in simulation; real-world transfer remains to be validated, though LIBERO-Plus is a recognized benchmark at robotics venues.\n387\t\n388\t\\paragraph{Future work.}\n389\tExtending CGS to capture semantic uncertainty (e.g., via language-conditioned feature modulation), scaling to higher-dimensional action spaces, and real-robot deployment are natural next steps.\n390\t\n391\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n392\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n393\t\\bibitem{liu2024libero} B.~Liu et al., ``LIBERO: Benchmarking knowledge transfer for lifelong robot learning,'' in \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n394\t\\bibitem{liberoplus2025} LIBERO-Plus Team, ``LIBERO-Plus: Robustness benchmarking for vision-language-action models,'' 2025.\n395\t\\bibitem{srpo2025} SRPO Authors, ``Self-refined policy optimization for robust VLA deployment,'' 2025.\n396\t\\bibitem{reconvla2025} ReconVLA Authors, ``Reconsidering VLA uncertainty for robust sample selection,'' 2025.\n397\t\\bibitem{safe2025} SAFE Authors, ``SAFE: Anomaly detection for safe VLA deployment,'' 2025.\n398\t\\end{thebibliography}\n399\t\n400\t\\end{document}\n401\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9MWD8xUBVCyK2j19WEhKH","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"3ad8eb31-0ea2-4ecf-a044-59d87057c54f","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:07:08.554Z"}
