{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GoucxMSVMFvH3rb9siFj9W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VHgg2gQB3y6fNUAVwVZ429","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T015051/median/review_report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22644,"cache_read_input_tokens":25231,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":22644,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SF4MGWJMnDiiipbE2UyZTq","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"0e6d19c1-eeec-469c-8024-447b585e41a1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VHgg2gQB3y6fNUAVwVZ429","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Dream Review Report — ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models\n2\t\n3\t**Dream ID:** 20260513T015051 | **Tier:** median | **Review Mode:** full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)  \n4\t**Venue:** CoRL 2026  \n5\t**Overall Score:** Borderline Reject (4.5/10)\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n10\t\n11\t### Reviewer 1 (R1) — Methods & Experiments\n12\t\n13\t#### Summary\n14\tACR-VLA proposes an inference-time framework augmenting flow-matching VLAs with conformal prediction machinery. The system draws K=10 samples, estimates per-DOF uncertainty via Ledoit-Wolf-shrunk covariance, adapts coverage thresholds online using chunk overlap (COC), and applies a 131K-parameter learned residual corrector (CRM) conditioned on 45D conformal features. Reports +6pp on SmolVLA and +1.9pp on SRPO on LIBERO-Plus.\n15\t\n16\t#### Strengths\n17\t- **S1:** Principled uncertainty decomposition — the 45D feature vector follows from multivariate Gaussian structure. Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage is well-justified for small-sample covariance. Ablation A4 (scalar: −2pp) provides direct evidence that structure matters.\n18\t- **S2:** Supervision-free ACI adaptation via COC — practically important for deployment without environment feedback. Ablation A1 confirms +1.5pp contribution.\n19\t- **S3:** Lightweight deployment footprint — CRM at 131K parameters is negligible; K=10 parallel samples are embarrassingly parallel; ~8% pipeline overhead is credible.\n20\t\n21\t#### Weaknesses\n22\t- **W1 (Critical):** K=10 is too small for reliable 45D covariance estimation (ratio ~0.22). Heavy Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage toward identity attenuates the off-diagonal entries contributing 18% of feature importance. No sensitivity analysis over K ∈ {5, 10, 20, 50}. Coverage guarantees under K=10 i.i.d. flow-matching samples are unverified.\n23\t- **W2 (Major):** COC correlation (ρ=0.40, 62% per-step accuracy) is too weak to carry the ACI framing. ρ=0.40 explains ~16% of variance. No empirical coverage rates reported. The \"conformal\" guarantee does not propagate through a noisy proxy signal.\n24\t- **W3 (Major):** CRM training regime systematically advantages augmentation-covered perturbations. Table 4 shows camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. Headline +6pp overstates generalization. Nominal −2pp degradation is unanalyzed (no per-task breakdown).\n25\t- **W4 (Major):** SRPO stacking result (+1.9pp) falls below the paper's own 2pp significance threshold. No error bars, confidence intervals, or seed counts on any result — a critical omission for robotics benchmarks.\n26\t\n27\t#### Questions for Authors\n28\t- Q1: What is the actual Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage coefficient for K=10? If >0.8, the covariance entries are essentially noise.\n29\t- Q2: What is the empirical coverage rate of steps flagged by ACI? What fraction correspond to actual failure?\n30\t- Q3: Is the −2pp nominal degradation concentrated in specific tasks? How many seeds for the SRPO experiment?\n31\t\n32\t**Method Soundness:** 5/10 — Sound engineering with identifiable theoretical gaps. Conformal framing overstates what is actually guaranteed.\n33\t\n34\t**Experimental Rigor:** 3/10 — No error bars on any result. SRPO result below stated significance. One benchmark family, two base VLAs. No K sensitivity analysis. No comparison to simpler uncertainty baselines (ensemble voting, MC dropout).\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t### Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty & Related Work\n39\t\n40\t#### Summary\n41\tACR-VLA combines conformal generative scoring (CGS), adaptive conformal inference with chunk overlap consistency (ACI+COC), and a lightweight residual correction module (CRM). Results show meaningful gains on a weaker base model (+6pp SmolVLA) but modest gains on a stronger one (+1.9pp SRPO), with in-distribution degradation.\n42\t\n43\t#### Strengths\n44\t- **S1:** Self-aware presentation — the paper honestly reports feature importance results that undermine the covariance motivation, the weak COC correlation, and nominal degradation. This transparency is rare and commendable.\n45\t- **S2:** Supervision-free uncertainty proxy (COC) — addresses a genuine deployment constraint. Meaningful engineering contribution even if individual ingredients are standard.\n46\t- **S3:** Comprehensive ablation design (A1–A7) — systematically isolates each contribution, including the honest A7 showing combined features yield only +0.5pp over epistemic-only.\n47\t\n48\t#### Weaknesses\n49\t- **W1 (Critical):** CGS novelty is minimal — Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage is textbook statistics (2004). The 45D feature vector is feature engineering, not a methodological contribution. Feature importance (72% marginal, 18% covariance) strongly suggests the multivariate CP formulation is unnecessary — per-DOF univariate CP would likely perform similarly and would be much simpler.\n50\t- **W2 (Critical):** The \"conformal\" framing is misleading for CRM. CP guarantees apply to the prediction set, not to downstream corrected actions. Once CGS features are fed into CRM, the coverage guarantee is severed. CP is used purely as a feature extractor. The module name \"Conformal Refinement Module\" is misleading — CRM does not implement conformal prediction.\n51\t- **W3 (Major):** CRM is architecturally indistinct from A2C2/PLD with different input features. A 2-layer MLP residual corrector with uncertainty conditioning is the same computational structure. The distinction is an input feature choice, not architectural innovation. No controlled comparison where A2C2 is augmented with the same uncertainty features.\n52\t- **W4 (Major):** Claim calibration inconsistency — abstract leads with \"+6pp\" on weaker SmolVLA; SRPO stacking (+1.9pp) is below significance. LIBERO-Plus may be a custom benchmark lacking independent validation.\n53\t\n54\t#### Questions for Authors\n55\t- Q1: Have you compared against per-DOF univariate CP intervals concatenated (much simpler than multivariate CP)? If results are similar, the multivariate framing is unnecessary overhead.\n56\t- Q2: What distributional guarantee does the corrected action inherit from CGS? Can you state a formal end-to-end guarantee, or is CP purely a feature extractor?\n57\t- Q3: Is the SRPO tradeoff (+1.9pp LIBERO-Plus, −0.5pp nominal) acceptable? Is there an ACI threshold calibration that recovers nominal performance?\n58\t\n59\t**Novelty Assessment:** 4/10 — CGS: standard statistics applied to flow-matching (incremental). COC: straightforward consistency check (modest). CRM: existing residual architecture with different inputs (incremental). No individual component advances the methodological frontier.\n60\t\n61\t**Related Work Completeness:** 6/10 — Core robotics literature covered. Gaps: ensemble-based uncertainty methods in robotics, temporal consistency in sequential prediction, recent CP for sequential decision-making (Lindemann et al., Lekeufack et al.), and clearer articulation of how ReconVLA's reconstruction distance differs from sample-spread.\n62\t\n63\t---\n64\t\n65\t## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n66\t\n67\t**Points of agreement:**\n68\t1. Both reviewers agree K=10 covariance estimation is problematic and under-justified.\n69\t2. Both flag the absence of error bars/confidence intervals as a critical experimental deficiency.\n70\t3. Both note the SRPO stacking result (+1.9pp) is below significance — the paper's strongest practical claim is weakest empirically.\n71\t4. Both credit the paper's honest self-assessment and transparent limitations.\n72\t5. Both identify that CRM's value derives primarily from per-DOF marginals (72%), undermining the multivariate CP motivation.\n73\t6. Both note the conformal framing overstates what is actually guaranteed.\n74\t\n75\t**Complementary concerns:**\n76\t- R1 emphasizes experimental gaps: no K sensitivity, no per-task nominal degradation breakdown, no simpler uncertainty baselines.\n77\t- R2 emphasizes novelty gaps: CGS is feature engineering, CRM is architecturally standard, the \"conformal\" branding is misleading when guarantees don't propagate.\n78\t\n79\t**No disagreements between reviewers on major points.**\n80\t\n81\t---\n82\t\n83\t## Step 3: Convergence\n84\t\n85\tAfter cross-sharing, both reviewers converge on the following assessment:\n86\t\n87\t### Core Contribution\n88\tThe paper presents a reasonable **engineering framework** — wrapping a VLA with uncertainty-conditioned residual correction works and produces real gains (+6pp on SmolVLA). The ablations are well-designed and honestly presented. COC as a supervision-free proxy is a practical contribution.\n89\t\n90\t### Core Deficiencies\n91\t1. **Novelty is insufficient for a top venue.** Each component (Ledoit-Wolf covariance, conformal scoring, MLP residual, chunk overlap consistency) is individually standard. The combination works but doesn't advance methodology.\n92\t2. **The conformal framing is overstated.** CP guarantees don't propagate through CRM. The paper's own feature importance analysis shows covariance structure (the part requiring multivariate CP) contributes only 18%. Per-DOF univariate CP is not compared.\n93\t3. **Experimental rigor is weak.** No error bars, no K sensitivity, no simpler baselines, one benchmark family, two base VLAs. The SRPO result is statistically indistinguishable from noise.\n94\t4. **Gains are narrow.** Strong improvements only on augmentation-covered perturbations (camera, noise: +9–10pp). Semantic perturbations (layout, language: +1–2pp) are barely affected. The headline +6pp average obscures this.\n95\t\n96\t### What Would Strengthen the Paper\n97\t- Per-DOF univariate CP baseline (the simplest test of whether multivariate CP is needed)\n98\t- K sensitivity analysis ({5, 10, 20, 50})\n99\t- Error bars across 3+ seeds on all results\n100\t- A third base VLA (e.g., OpenVLA, Octo) and a second benchmark (e.g., RLBench)\n101\t- Reframe away from \"conformal guarantees\" toward \"uncertainty-conditioned residual correction\"\n102\t- Gating mechanism to eliminate the −2pp nominal degradation\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n107\t\n108\t### Scores\n109\t\n110\t| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n111\t|-----------|-------|---------------|\n112\t| Novelty | 4/10 | Standard components combined; no methodological advance |\n113\t| Method Soundness | 5/10 | Engineering works but conformal framing overstates guarantees |\n114\t| Experimental Rigor | 3/10 | No error bars, no K sensitivity, one benchmark, sub-threshold SRPO result |\n115\t| Related Work | 6/10 | Core coverage adequate; missing ensemble/CP-for-RL literature |\n116\t| Clarity & Honesty | 8/10 | Commendably transparent about limitations and feature importance |\n117\t| Significance | 4/10 | +6pp on weak baseline is useful but narrow; +1.9pp on strong baseline is not significant |\n118\t\n119\t### Overall: 4.5/10 — Borderline Reject\n120\t\n121\t### Recommendation\n122\t**Reject** for CoRL 2026 in current form.\n123\t\n124\t### Key Reasons\n125\t1. The novelty bar is not met: each component is standard, and the multivariate CP formulation — the paper's central claim — is undermined by the authors' own feature importance analysis showing covariance contributes only 18%.\n126\t2. Experimental rigor is insufficient: no error bars on any result, the signature SRPO stacking result falls below the paper's own significance threshold, and only one benchmark family is tested.\n127\t3. The conformal framing is misleading: CP guarantees do not propagate through the learned CRM, making the \"conformal\" branding aspirational rather than substantive.\n128\t\n129\t### What the Authors Got Right\n130\t- Honest, transparent presentation of limitations and negative results\n131\t- Well-designed ablation suite (A1–A7) that enables independent assessment of each component\n132\t- COC as a supervision-free ACI signal is a practical contribution worth developing further\n133\t- The engineering works: +6pp on SmolVLA is a real improvement for practitioners\n134\t\n135\t### Path to Acceptance\n136\tA revised version could become competitive by: (1) reframing from \"conformal guarantees\" to \"uncertainty-conditioned residual correction,\" (2) adding univariate CP and ensemble baselines to justify multivariate CP, (3) providing error bars and K sensitivity, (4) testing on a second benchmark and third base VLA, and (5) adding a learned gating mechanism to eliminate nominal degradation. The core idea has engineering value — the presentation and validation need to catch up.\n137\t\n138\t---\n139\t\n140\t*Review conducted by: Dream Review Director (2 Reviewers, full roundtable protocol)*  \n141\t*Date: 2026-05-13*\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SF4MGWJMnDiiipbE2UyZTq","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"26346b8d-e2a6-474a-b019-015a7e23bb60","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:07:40.021Z"}
