{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a9bcbaccc135e124a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9MWD8xUBVCyK2j19WEhKH","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":69170,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":154209},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"69a579c1-b87a-4610-98d7-bc084bc6e95b","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TvNgLocAk4PoZPgXYy2aje","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JybviesceuByJsj3AbZxvw","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"acr-vla-conformal-refinement","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260513T023849","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"\n# R1 Review: ACR-VLA — Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Focus areas: Methods and Experiments**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes ACR-VLA, an inference-time module that draws K=10 samples from a flow-matching VLA, extracts 45-dimensional per-DOF conformal geometry statistics (CGS) using Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, and feeds them into a lightweight 131K-parameter Conformal Refinement Module (CRM) to produce dimension-selective residual action corrections. An Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) mechanism driven by a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) signal maintains calibrated coverage without external supervision. On LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves SmolVLA by +15.0pp and, when stacked on SRPO, achieves 88.7% (new SOTA). Seven ablations and several supplementary experiments support three claims about per-DOF features, COC signal quality, and orthogonality with training-time methods.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured ablation design.** The ablation suite (A1-A7) is carefully designed to isolate each component's contribution. The clean gradient none (60.8%) < scalar (66.8%) < per-DOF (72.8%) across A3/A4/full is the strongest evidence for Claim 1 and mirrors the MVP result, providing internal consistency across experiments at different scales.\n\n- **Principled statistical methodology for low-K regime.** The use of Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage at K=10, d=7 (p/n=0.7) is well-motivated. The paper correctly identifies that raw sample covariance is ill-conditioned in this regime and provides concrete condition number reduction (10^4 to 10^2). This is a methodologically sound choice that distinguishes the work from naive multi-sample approaches.\n\n- **COC is a genuinely novel contribution.** Using inter-chunk prediction consistency as a self-referential ACI signal is creative and practically important. The rho=0.61 correlation with task success is modest but honest, and the 2.5x convergence speedup over episode-level ACI (47 vs 120 episodes) is practically significant. The fact that asymptotic performance is equivalent (72.8 vs 72.1) but convergence is much faster makes the value proposition clear.\n\n- **Honest per-perturbation breakdown.** Table 4 transparently shows where ACR-VLA works and where it does not. The language perturbation result (+2.1pp, p=0.31) is correctly flagged as non-significant. The gain gradient (camera > noise > robot-state > lighting > background > layout > language) is physically interpretable and aligns well with which perturbation types should alter action-space covariance structure.\n\n- **Orthogonality claim is well-supported.** The +6.6pp gain when stacking ACR-VLA on SRPO (82.1% -> 88.7%) provides concrete evidence that inference-time conformal correction addresses different failure modes than training-time RL. The nominal LIBERO preservation (97.8% vs 98.1%) is reassuring.\n\n- **Practical overhead is minimal.** 12.4ms overhead and 131K parameters make this genuinely deployable. The SnapFlow integration (4.9x speedup, -1.6pp) further addresses latency concerns.\n\n- **Feature importance analysis provides mechanism evidence.** The perturbation-specific top features (camera -> conformal_radius_x, noise -> std_z, robot-state -> conformal_radius_joint3) confirm that CRM performs genuinely dimension-selective correction rather than learning a generic bias.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **All LIBERO-Plus results are imagined / \"best-tier\" projections, not actual experimental results.** This is the most critical issue. The project status shows that only the MVP point-reaching experiment has been completed (status: DONE). The full pipeline experiment (plan_001) is listed as PREPARING. All main results (Table 2), all ablations (Table 3), the SRPO stacking, COC validation, and every other result beyond the MVP are generated from the \"imagined_experiments\" framework under the \"best\" tier. The paper presents these projected numbers as if they were actual experimental results. This fundamentally undermines every claim. The review_context.yaml explicitly states the \"expected_results\" match what the paper reports — because the paper was written *from* those expected results, not from actual data. **No amount of ablation design quality can compensate for the absence of real experiments.**\n\n- **K=10 justification is insufficient.** The paper uses K=10 throughout but never ablates over K. At p/n=0.7, even with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, the covariance estimate has substantial error. The MVP shows ~15pp degradation from oracle to K=10 estimated covariance — this is a large gap. Critical missing experiments: (a) K=5, K=15, K=20 ablation on the full pipeline to characterize the performance-vs-compute tradeoff; (b) analysis of how CGS feature quality degrades at lower K; (c) theoretical or empirical characterization of the minimum K required for the Mahalanobis distance to be meaningful. The paper_framing.md acknowledges this as an anticipated reviewer concern but proposes only a K=20 ablation \"if reviewers push\" — this should be in the main paper.\n\n- **CRM training confound with synthetic augmentation.** The CRM is trained on demonstrations augmented with 5 synthetic perturbation types (camera, noise, robot-state, lighting, background). This creates a confound: is the CRM learning to correct based on conformal features, or is it memorizing perturbation-specific correction patterns seen during training? The per-perturbation breakdown (Table 4) is consistent with this concern — the largest gains are precisely on perturbation types covered by synthetic augmentation (camera +19.5, noise +17.8), while uncovered types show smaller gains (layout +5.2, language +2.1). A critical missing control is CRM tested on perturbation types *not seen during training* — e.g., train without camera augmentation and test on camera perturbations — to disentangle \"conformal features are informative\" from \"CRM memorized augmentation-specific corrections.\"\n\n- **Single base model (SmolVLA) limits generality claims.** The paper claims ACR-VLA works with \"any flow-matching VLA\" but tests only on SmolVLA. The SRPO stacking uses the same SmolVLA backbone. Testing on at least one additional VLA architecture (e.g., OpenVLA, Octo, or pi0) is necessary to support the generality claim. Different VLAs may produce flow-matching samples with very different covariance structures — the 45D feature design assumes this structure is consistent and informative across architectures.\n\n- **No confidence intervals or variance estimates on key results.** Tables 2-4 report point estimates without standard deviations, confidence intervals, or number of evaluation episodes. For success rate differences of 3-6pp (e.g., A1 vs full = 4.6pp, A5 vs full = 3.4pp), this is essential. How many episodes per condition? What is the standard error? The p-values in Table 4 suggest some statistical testing was done, but the methodology is not described (what test? how many seeds? bootstrap?). The ablation gaps could be within noise.\n\n### Minor-Major\n\n- **45D feature vector dimensionality is inconsistently described.** The abstract and method section say \"45-dimensional\" (7 DOFs x {std, conformal_radius} + shared ellipsoid_volume = 15). But the paper also mentions \"15-dimensional feature vector per DOF ... spanning 2-3 timesteps\" and the decisions.yaml says \"CGS 40D\" with \"action_dim=6, H_eff=5\". The actual architecture uses action_dim=6 (not 7 — gripper is excluded?) and H_eff=5. So is it 6 DOFs x 5 steps x {std, radius} + 5 volumes = 65D? Or 6 x {std, radius} + 1 volume = 13D? The 45D number appears fixed across all documents but the underlying dimensionality arithmetic is unclear and contradictory.\n\n- **COC formulation is underspecified.** Equation (1) defines COC but several details are missing: What is the overlap window size? How many timesteps overlap between consecutive chunks given chunk_size=50 and execution horizon H_eff=5? The denominator uses the norm of the previous chunk's overlap — what happens when this is near zero (e.g., near-stationary actions)? The epsilon term is mentioned but not specified. COC is used both as an ACI update signal and implicitly as a coverage indicator, but the mapping from continuous COC to binary coverage events (the indicator in Eq. 2) is not explained.\n\n- **ACI update rule (Eq. 2) has a conceptual issue.** The standard ACI update uses 1[Y_{t+1} not in C_t] as the coverage indicator. Equation 2 uses 1[COC_t < q_hat_t], which conflates the conformal threshold with a COC threshold. This is not standard ACI — it is a heuristic adaptation. The paper should be explicit about this departure and discuss when/why it preserves the coverage guarantee (or acknowledge that it does not provide finite-sample guarantees, only asymptotic).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Which results are from actual experiments vs. imagined projections?** The project metadata indicates only the MVP point-reaching experiment has been run. Please clarify the experimental status of every table and figure in the paper.\n\n2. **How does CRM performance change on perturbation types excluded from training augmentation?** Specifically: if you train CRM without camera augmentation, what is the gain on camera perturbations? This would disentangle conformal feature informativeness from augmentation memorization.\n\n3. **What is the actual CGS feature dimensionality?** The paper says 45D throughout, but the architecture decisions say action_dim=6 and H_eff=5 yielding 40D. The text says d=7 DOFs. Which is correct?\n\n4. **How many evaluation episodes per condition, and across how many seeds?** Please provide standard deviations for all results in Tables 2-4. For ablation gaps of 3-6pp, statistical significance is not self-evident.\n\n5. **Why no K ablation?** Given that the oracle-to-K=10 degradation is ~15pp in the MVP, characterizing performance vs. K is essential. What happens at K=5? K=20?\n\n6. **How does ACR-VLA perform on a second VLA backbone?** Even a single additional architecture would substantially strengthen the generality claim.\n\n7. **What is the COC-to-coverage mapping in the ACI update?** Equation 2 uses an indicator 1[COC_t < q_hat_t] but q_hat_t is supposed to be a conformal quantile (action-space Mahalanobis distance), not a COC threshold. Are there two separate thresholds? How are they connected?\n\n8. **What is the calibration set for static calibration (A1)?** Is it drawn from LIBERO nominal demonstrations? If so, the 0.78 coverage on LIBERO-Plus could partly reflect calibration set mismatch rather than the inherent limitation of static calibration.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Minor Issues\n\n- **Table numbering inconsistency.** The paper text references Tables 1-8 but the actual paper contains Tables 1-8 with different numbering from the experiment plan. Table 1 in the plan is \"LIBERO & LIBERO-Plus Success Rate\" but Table 1 in the paper is \"MVP point-reaching.\" Confusing.\n\n- **Bibliography is incomplete.** Only 5 references are listed. Many claims reference methods (A2C2, UPS, CP-in-the-Loop, DiU, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow, SnapFlow) that are not cited. For a CoRL submission this would be unacceptable.\n\n- **\"15-dimensional feature vector per DOF\"** (Section 3.1) is confusing. A single DOF should have a fixed number of features (std + conformal radius = 2, plus shared volume). \"15-dimensional per DOF\" suggests 15 features per DOF, which for 7 DOFs would be 105D, not 45D.\n\n- **The paper uses \"pp\" (percentage points) inconsistently.** Some places use \"pp\" and others use \"%\" for the same type of quantity (absolute success rate differences).\n\n- **Section 3.1 says d=7 DOFs (3 translation + 3 rotation + 1 gripper)** but decisions.yaml says action_dim=6 (gripper excluded from CGS). This should be clarified.\n\n- **No figures in the paper.** The experiment plan lists 14 main figures and 5 supplementary figures, but the submitted paper contains zero figures. For a methods paper about geometry-aware uncertainty, visual illustrations (ellipsoid visualizations, convergence curves, feature importance heatmaps) would substantially improve clarity and impact.\n\n- **Equation formatting.** K{=}10 uses unusual LaTeX formatting throughout. Standard $K=10$ would be more readable.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 5/10 | The methodological framework (CGS + ACI/COC + CRM) is well-conceived and the statistical choices (Ledoit-Wolf, joint Mahalanobis) are principled. However, the results are projections rather than actual experiments, the ACI update rule departs from standard theory without acknowledgment, and critical confounds (augmentation memorization, K sensitivity) are uncontrolled. |\n| **Empirical Quality** | 3/10 | Only the MVP experiment appears to have been actually conducted. All LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus results are imagined under a \"best-case\" scenario. No confidence intervals, no seed variance, no statistical test descriptions. The ablation *design* is excellent (7 ablations with clear isolation), but the numbers filling those ablations are projections, not data. |\n| **Clarity** | 5/10 | The writing is generally clear and well-organized. However, the CGS dimensionality is contradictory (45D vs 40D vs unclear), COC is underspecified, the ACI update rule conflates two thresholds, the bibliography is stub-level, and there are no figures despite extensive planned visualizations. |\n| **Significance** | 6/10 | The core idea — using per-DOF conformal statistics as learned correction features — is novel and potentially impactful for the VLA robustness community. If the experimental results hold, this would be a meaningful contribution to CoRL. The COC signal is a creative secondary contribution. However, significance is conditional on the results being real. |\n| **Overall Recommendation** | 4/10 | The paper presents a strong methodological idea with a well-designed experiment plan, but the experimental results appear to be projections rather than actual data. If all results were real and came with proper statistical reporting, this would be a 6-7 paper. In its current form, it cannot be accepted. The authors should run the actual experiments, add confidence intervals, address the K ablation, test on a second VLA backbone, and add the missing figures and references. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I have carefully examined the paper, the experiment plan, the imagined experiment specifications, and the project metadata. The project status clearly shows only MVP is DONE while plan_001 (the core experiment) is PREPARING. I am confident in my assessment of the methodological strengths and experimental gaps. |\n\n---\n\n## Summary Assessment\n\nACR-VLA proposes an elegant and well-motivated method for inference-time action correction using structured conformal statistics. The per-DOF conformal feature idea is novel, the ablation design is thorough, and the COC signal is a creative contribution. However, the paper's fatal flaw is that the experimental results beyond the MVP appear to be best-case projections rather than actual data — the project metadata confirms the main pipeline experiment is still in PREPARING status. Additionally, even setting aside the provenance question, the paper lacks: (a) K sensitivity analysis, (b) a held-out perturbation type control for the augmentation confound, (c) a second VLA backbone, (d) statistical reporting (CIs, seeds, tests), (e) any figures, and (f) a complete bibliography. The idea deserves to be developed into a real paper with real experiments.\n\n**Recommendation: Reject in current form, with encouragement to resubmit once actual experiments are completed.**\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":684,"cache_read_input_tokens":67781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":684,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9MWD8xUBVCyK2j19WEhKH","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"33a6d791-79e7-449b-9b34-58a487769100"}
