{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F9MWD8xUBVCyK2j19WEhKH","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the R1 review:\n\n**Overall score: 4/10 (borderline reject)**\n\nThe review identifies ACR-VLA as a methodologically well-conceived paper with a novel core idea (per-DOF conformal statistics as learned correction features) and a thorough ablation design (A1-A7). The COC signal is a creative secondary contribution.\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Clean ablation gradient (none < scalar < per-DOF) consistent with MVP\n- Principled Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for low-K regime\n- Honest per-perturbation breakdown with transparent limitations\n- Strong orthogonality evidence via SRPO stacking\n\n**Critical weaknesses raised:**\n1. **Results provenance**: All results beyond MVP are best-case projections from the imagined_experiments framework, not actual experimental data. Project status confirms plan_001 is PREPARING.\n2. **No K ablation**: K=10 is used throughout without sensitivity analysis despite ~15pp oracle-to-estimated degradation in MVP.\n3. **CRM training confound**: Gains correlate with augmentation coverage, raising the question of augmentation memorization vs. genuine conformal feature utility. Missing control: held-out perturbation types.\n4. **Single VLA backbone**: Only SmolVLA tested despite generality claims.\n5. **No statistical reporting**: No confidence intervals, seed variance, or test methodology descriptions on any result.\n6. **Missing figures, incomplete bibliography, CGS dimensionality contradictions** (45D vs 40D vs paper_framing descriptions).\n\nThe review recommends rejection in current form with encouragement to resubmit once actual experiments are completed, K sensitivity is characterized, and proper statistical reporting is added.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T023849/best/review_report.md`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9bcbaccc135e124a (use SendMessage with to: 'a9bcbaccc135e124a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 73253\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 168352</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"8abac22d-b139-439b-87bd-43b65081801f","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:09:35.738Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning)\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below)\n- Acceptance rate historically ~25-35%\n- CoRL values: robotics relevance, empirical rigor, real-world applicability, honest limitations\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: An inference-time module for flow-matching VLAs that extracts per-DOF conformal statistics from K=10 samples (CGS: 45D features with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage) and feeds them into a lightweight CRM (131K params, ~12ms overhead) for dimension-selective action correction. Uses Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) driven by a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) signal (self-referential, no external supervision).\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. Per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver (12pp ablation gap over generic residual)\n2. COC provides effective self-referential signal for ACI (ρ=0.61, 2.5x faster convergence)\n3. Orthogonal to training-time methods (stacked on SRPO → 88.7% LIBERO-Plus SOTA)\n\n**Main results (Table 2)**:\n- Base SmolVLA: 97.3% LIBERO / 57.8% LIBERO-Plus\n- ACR-VLA: 96.5% LIBERO / 72.8% LIBERO-Plus (+15.0pp, 12.4ms overhead)\n- SRPO: 98.1% LIBERO / 82.1% LIBERO-Plus\n- ACR-VLA+SRPO: 97.8% LIBERO / 88.7% LIBERO-Plus (+6.6pp)\n\n**Ablations (Table 3)**:\n- A1 (no ACI, static calib): 68.2% → ACI contributes 4.6pp\n- A2 (no CRM, sample selection only): 63.5% → CRM learned correction worth 9.3pp over selection\n- A3 (CRM w/o conformal features): 60.8% → conformal features contribute 12.0pp\n- A4 (CRM w/ scalar uncertainty): 66.8% → per-DOF > scalar by 6.0pp\n\n**Additional experiments**:\n- Per-perturbation breakdown (Table 4): gains on 6/7 types (camera +19.5, noise +17.8, robot-state +16.2, lighting +13.4, background +11.8, layout +5.2; language +2.1 not significant)\n- COC validation (Table 5): ρ=0.61, p=3e-5; ACI coverage 0.89 vs static 0.78; 28% volume narrowing\n- A5 (CGS vs Bonferroni): joint Mahalanobis beats Bonferroni by 3.4pp, Bonferroni 35% larger volumes\n- A6 (COC vs episode-level ACI): similar final perf (72.8 vs 72.1), COC 2.5x faster convergence, +4.2pp early\n- A7 (epistemic vs aleatoric): CGS 45D (72.8%) >> DiU 7D (63.9%), combined 52D only +0.7pp more\n- Feature importance: conformal radius 48%, std 31%, ellipsoid volume 21%; perturbation-specific top features differ\n- SnapFlow: 71.2% at 38ms (4.9x speedup), 87% diversity retained\n- MVP point-reaching: per-DOF 0.0735 < scalar 0.0788 < none 0.0882\n\n**Experiment design context** (from review_context.yaml — the full experiment plan):\nThe paper covers all 14 planned experiments. The context lists expected results that match what the paper reports. Planned tables/figures include:\n- Tables 1-8 plus S1-S5 (supplementary)\n- Figures 1-14 plus S1-S5\nHowever, the 7-page paper only shows Tables 1-8 inline; the supplementary figures/tables are referenced but appear to be in supplementary material not included in this PDF.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences, your own words)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methodological soundness and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps, experimental concerns, missing controls)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues** (presentation, notation, etc.)\n6. **Scores**: Rate each dimension 1-10:\n   - Soundness (methodological rigor)\n   - Empirical quality (experimental coverage, statistical rigor)\n   - Clarity\n   - Significance\n   - Overall recommendation\n   - Confidence (1-5, 5=expert)\n\nBe critical but fair. Flag issues with statistical methodology, missing baselines, potential confounds, and experimental gaps. Check whether the ablations truly isolate the claimed contributions. Consider whether K=10 is well-justified, whether the CRM training procedure introduces confounds, and whether LIBERO-Plus alone is sufficient for the claims made.","agentId":"a9bcbaccc135e124a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the R1 review:\n\n**Overall score: 4/10 (borderline reject)**\n\nThe review identifies ACR-VLA as a methodologically well-conceived paper with a novel core idea (per-DOF conformal statistics as learned correction features) and a thorough ablation design (A1-A7). The COC signal is a creative secondary contribution.\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Clean ablation gradient (none < scalar < per-DOF) consistent with MVP\n- Principled Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for low-K regime\n- Honest per-perturbation breakdown with transparent limitations\n- Strong orthogonality evidence via SRPO stacking\n\n**Critical weaknesses raised:**\n1. **Results provenance**: All results beyond MVP are best-case projections from the imagined_experiments framework, not actual experimental data. Project status confirms plan_001 is PREPARING.\n2. **No K ablation**: K=10 is used throughout without sensitivity analysis despite ~15pp oracle-to-estimated degradation in MVP.\n3. **CRM training confound**: Gains correlate with augmentation coverage, raising the question of augmentation memorization vs. genuine conformal feature utility. Missing control: held-out perturbation types.\n4. **Single VLA backbone**: Only SmolVLA tested despite generality claims.\n5. **No statistical reporting**: No confidence intervals, seed variance, or test methodology descriptions on any result.\n6. **Missing figures, incomplete bibliography, CGS dimensionality contradictions** (45D vs 40D vs paper_framing descriptions).\n\nThe review recommends rejection in current form with encouragement to resubmit once actual experiments are completed, K sensitivity is characterized, and proper statistical reporting is added.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T023849/best/review_report.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":168352,"totalTokens":73253,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4325,"cache_read_input_tokens":68465,"output_tokens":462,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4325},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":462,"cache_read_input_tokens":68465,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4325,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a1d1276eb5b3910a3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SF4MGWJMnDiiipbE2UyZTq","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":57328,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":175848},"uuid":"10cdebbc-8f2e-4111-be90-82038fc6bd63","session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SF4MGWJMnDiiipbE2UyZTq","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI have now thoroughly reviewed the paper draft (`paper.tex`), the IDEA_BRIEF with its extensive landscape analysis and 30+ references, the paper_framing.md, memory.md, decisions.yaml, and both prior dream review reports (best-tier: Weak Accept 5/10; median-tier: Borderline Reject 4.5/10). I am ready to deliver my R2 review focused on Novelty and Related Work.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nACR-VLA proposes an inference-time correction module for flow-matching VLAs that extracts 45-dimensional per-DOF conformal statistics (CGS) from K=10 action samples, adapts conformal thresholds online via a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency signal (ACI+COC), and feeds these features into a lightweight 131K-parameter MLP (CRM) to produce dimension-selective residual corrections. The paper reports +15pp on base SmolVLA and a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA of 88.7% when stacked on SRPO. The core novelty claim is that no prior work uses structured multivariate conformal statistics as input features for a learned action correction module.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Precise gap identification and positioning.** The paper correctly maps a genuine unoccupied niche: ReconVLA uses scalar CP for sample selection (cannot synthesize better actions), CP-in-the-Loop closes the CP-planning loop but through optimization not learned correction, UPS uses CP for strategy gating without feeding conformal features into the residual policy, and A2C2/PLD learn residual corrections without calibrated uncertainty conditioning. The differentiation from each of these five closest works is accurate and specific. This is better positioning than most CoRL submissions achieve.\n\n- **Clean ablation gradient validating the core claim.** The none (60.8%) < scalar (66.8%) < per-DOF (72.8%) hierarchy across A3/A4/Full is the paper's strongest novelty evidence. The 12pp gap between CRM-without-conformal-features and full ACR-VLA is large enough to be convincing even without error bars. This ablation directly addresses the question \"do conformal features matter or would any residual policy suffice?\" and the answer is clear.\n\n- **Epistemic vs. aleatoric distinction (A7) is well-argued.** The comparison with DiU-based aleatoric features (63.9% vs. 72.8%) provides principled justification for why multi-sample epistemic uncertainty is the right signal for distribution-shift correction. The argument that aleatoric uncertainty is elevated even in-distribution (causing over-intervention and -2.5pp nominal degradation) while epistemic uncertainty rises specifically under shift is theoretically sound and empirically validated. This is a genuine conceptual contribution.\n\n- **COC as self-referential ACI signal.** Exploiting the temporal overlap structure of action chunks for self-supervised coverage feedback is a creative idea that addresses a real deployment constraint (no external reward signal needed). The 2.5x convergence speedup (47 vs. 120 episodes) is practically meaningful. While temporal consistency checking is not new in principle, applying it as a conformal coverage proxy for receding-horizon action chunks is a novel instantiation.\n\n- **Honest per-perturbation breakdown.** Transparently reporting that language perturbations yield no significant gain (+2.1pp, p=0.31) and that gains correlate with synthetic augmentation coverage is commendable and rare. This honesty strengthens rather than weakens the paper.\n\n- **Feature importance analysis provides mechanistic evidence.** The perturbation-specific gradient patterns (camera activates position DOFs, robot-state activates joint DOFs) confirm that CRM genuinely learns dimension-selective behavior rather than uniform correction. This is emergent, not hard-coded, and is a meaningful empirical finding.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 (Critical): The reference list contains only 5 entries.** This is drastically inadequate for a paper that positions itself at the intersection of four active research areas (VLA robustness, conformal prediction, uncertainty quantification, residual policies). The IDEA_BRIEF cites 30+ works with full bibliographic details -- Gibbs & Candes (NeurIPS 2021), Braun et al. (arXiv:2507.20941), Barber et al. (Annals of Statistics 2023), Ledoit & Wolf (JMVA 2004), DiU (arXiv:2605.00941), A2C2 (arXiv:2509.23224), PLD (arXiv:2511.00091), UPS (arXiv:2602.22474), CP-in-the-Loop (arXiv:2510.16376), TT-VLA, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow, SnapFlow, CRT, ACG, and others. All are discussed in the Related Work section of the paper but none are formally cited. This is a fatal formatting deficiency: reviewers at any venue will flag a 5-reference paper as incomplete, and the missing citations make it impossible to verify the related work claims. Every method mentioned by name in the text must have a corresponding bibliography entry.\n\n- **W2 (Major): \"First to combine X with Y\" novelty is narrow and potentially overclaimed.** The claim \"first method to combine multivariate conformal prediction with learned residual correction\" is likely true but is an extremely specific conjunction. Each individual component is well-established: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage (2004), Mahalanobis-based conformal scoring (textbook), ACI (Gibbs & Candes 2021), learned residual correction (A2C2, PLD, general residual learning). The novelty is a systems-level integration with a particular feature engineering choice (45D CGS vector). The paper should be more forthright about this: the contribution is \"we show that feeding structured conformal features into a residual corrector yields large gains\" -- which is valuable -- rather than implying a methodological advance in either conformal prediction or residual learning independently. The framing should emphasize the empirical insight (per-DOF conformal features are surprisingly informative for correction) rather than the priority claim (first to combine X with Y).\n\n- **W3 (Major): No experimental comparison with the closest related methods.** A2C2, PLD, UPS, CP-in-the-Loop, DiG-Flow, RobustVLA, ReconVLA, and SAFE are all discussed in Related Work but none appear in experimental tables. The only baselines are base SmolVLA and SRPO. This is the most critical experimental gap for the novelty argument: without running A2C2 (which is architecturally identical to CRM minus conformal features -- i.e., ablation A3 approximates it but is not the same as running the actual method) and ReconVLA (the closest CP-based method), the reader cannot determine whether ACR-VLA's gains come from the specific conformal feature design or from general properties of any inference-time correction module applied to SmolVLA. A3 provides indirect evidence but running the actual published methods would be far more convincing.\n\n- **W4 (Major): The \"orthogonal gains\" claim (Claim 3) lacks falsification analysis.** The paper claims ACR-VLA is \"orthogonal\" to training-time methods based on the single observation that ACR-VLA+SRPO > SRPO. But orthogonality is a stronger claim than additive improvement. True orthogonality would require showing that the gain is preserved across multiple training-time methods (SRPO, DiG-Flow, RobustVLA) and that the failure modes corrected by ACR-VLA do not overlap with those corrected by SRPO. The paper provides no failure mode decomposition. What would falsify orthogonality? If ACR-VLA+DiG-Flow showed no gain over DiG-Flow, the \"orthogonal\" framing would collapse to \"complementary to SRPO specifically.\" Testing on a single training-time method is insufficient to support a general orthogonality claim.\n\n- **W5 (Moderate): COC novelty may be overstated.** Temporal consistency checking between overlapping predictions is a well-known idea in sequential prediction (e.g., temporal difference consistency in RL value estimation, multi-step prediction consistency in model-based RL). The specific application to action chunk overlap for conformal coverage is new, but the underlying principle -- \"consecutive predictions about the same future should agree\" -- is not. The paper should cite and differentiate from temporal consistency work more explicitly. Additionally, the COC-vs-episode-level ablation (A6) shows only 0.7pp final performance difference (72.1% vs 72.8%), meaning COC's primary advantage is convergence speed, not final quality. This should be stated more precisely.\n\n- **W6 (Moderate): Single base VLA and single benchmark limits generality claims.** All experiments use SmolVLA on LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus. The paper's language (\"any flow-matching VLA,\" \"orthogonal to training-time methods\") implies generality that is not empirically supported. Flow-matching VLAs differ substantially in architecture (pi0 uses a different action expert than SmolVLA), and LIBERO-Plus is one benchmark with specific perturbation types. Testing on at least one additional VLA (e.g., pi0 or OpenVLA) and ideally a second benchmark would substantially strengthen the generality argument.\n\n- **W7 (Minor): The \"conformal\" framing may be misleading.** Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees for the prediction set. Once CGS features are fed into CRM, the corrected action inherits no formal coverage guarantee. The paper does not claim end-to-end coverage, but the terminology (\"Conformal Refinement Module,\" \"conformal correction\") may mislead readers into thinking the corrected actions carry distributional guarantees. A clearer statement that CP is used as a feature extractor (not as a guarantor of the output) would improve intellectual honesty.\n\n- **W8 (Minor): Per-DOF univariate CP baseline is missing.** The paper compares multivariate CGS (Mahalanobis-based) against Bonferroni correction (A5, -3.4pp). But the most informative comparison would be per-DOF univariate CP intervals concatenated as features (simpler than multivariate, avoids Bonferroni conservatism in a different way). The feature importance analysis showing 72% from marginals (per-DOF std) and only ~18% from covariance structure suggests that most of CGS's value comes from the marginal features that univariate CP could also provide.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **References**: The bibliography contains only 5 entries. Is this a draft artifact? The Related Work text discusses 10+ methods by name without citations. Will the camera-ready include full references for all discussed work?\n\n2. **A2C2 comparison**: Ablation A3 (CRM without conformal features) is described as \"comparable to generic residual methods like A2C2.\" Have you actually run A2C2's published code on your setup? If A2C2 achieves, say, 65% (above A3's 60.8%), that would weaken the claim that conformal features are the sole differentiator.\n\n3. **Orthogonality falsification**: What result would cause you to retract the \"orthogonal to training-time methods\" claim? Have you tested stacking on DiG-Flow or RobustVLA in addition to SRPO?\n\n4. **Univariate CP features**: Have you compared against concatenated per-DOF univariate conformal intervals (not Bonferroni-corrected joint coverage, but simply per-DOF marginal CP intervals used as features)? Given that 72% of feature importance comes from marginals, this is the critical missing baseline for justifying the multivariate formulation.\n\n5. **End-to-end coverage guarantee**: Can you state a formal guarantee on the corrected action, or is CP purely a feature extractor in this pipeline? If the latter, would reframing as \"uncertainty-conditioned residual correction\" be more accurate than \"conformal refinement\"?\n\n6. **COC vs. temporal consistency literature**: How does COC relate to temporal difference consistency in RL or multi-step prediction consistency in model-based planning? What is specifically novel about using chunk overlap beyond the application domain?\n\n7. **Feature importance implications**: Your own gradient analysis shows covariance-related features contribute only ~18-21% of importance. Does this undermine the multivariate CP motivation? Would a simpler \"per-DOF std + scalar volume\" feature set (without Mahalanobis ellipsoids) achieve similar results?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- Table labels reference `\\ref{tab:aci}` but the label is placed after `\\label{tab:coc}` on the COC table, creating a mislabel.\n- The bibliography entries use placeholder author names (\"SRPO Authors,\" \"ReconVLA Authors\") -- these must be replaced with actual citations for any submission.\n- The paper discusses SnapFlow integration but does not cite SnapFlow in the bibliography.\n- Section 4.5 references \"Section~\\ref{sec:a5}\" for Bonferroni comparison but the ablation numbering in the text (A5) is introduced without a systematic ablation table entry -- A5 appears only in its own subsection, not in Table 3.\n- The CGS dimensionality is described as 45D in some places and the construction in Section 3.1 describes \"15-dimensional feature vector per DOF... plus scalar volume, totaling 45 dimensions\" which is arithmetically inconsistent (15 x 7 = 105, not 45). The actual construction from IDEA_BRIEF (7 per-DOF std + 7 conformal radii + 7 volumes = 21, or with H_eff steps = 30+5+5 = 40D) should be clarified.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Originality** | 5/10 | The specific combination (multivariate CP features as input to learned residual correction) is novel but narrow. Individual components are all established. The insight that per-DOF conformal features are informative for correction is valuable but is more empirical finding than methodological advance. |\n| **Related Work Coverage** | 3/10 | The textual discussion is adequate and accurate, but having only 5 bibliography entries in a paper that names 10+ related methods is unacceptable at any venue. The IDEA_BRIEF shows the authors know the literature well -- this knowledge must be reflected in proper citations. Missing experimental comparisons with closest methods (A2C2, ReconVLA, UPS) further weaken positioning. |\n| **Clarity** | 7/10 | The writing is clear and the story is logically coherent. The ablation structure is well-designed. Honest limitations discussion is appreciated. The 45D feature dimensionality description is internally inconsistent and needs fixing. |\n| **Significance** | 6/10 | +15pp on base SmolVLA and 88.7% LIBERO-Plus SOTA (if statistically significant) are meaningful results. However, gains are concentrated on augmentation-covered perturbations, single-benchmark evaluation limits impact, and the \"orthogonal gains\" claim is under-supported. Practical deployment value (12ms overhead, SnapFlow compatibility) is a genuine strength. |\n| **Overall Recommendation** | 5/10 | Borderline. The empirical results are promising and the ablation hierarchy is convincing. The paper identifies a real gap and fills it with a working system. However, the 5-reference bibliography is a fatal deficiency, the novelty is primarily in combination/feature engineering rather than methodology, closest baselines are absent from experiments, and the orthogonality claim needs broader validation. With proper citations, A2C2/ReconVLA baselines, a second base VLA, and honest reframing of the novelty (empirical insight > methodological advance), this could reach accept at CoRL. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I am familiar with the conformal prediction, residual policy, and VLA robustness literatures. I have verified the related work claims against the IDEA_BRIEF's landscape analysis. My main uncertainty is whether the reported numbers (particularly the 88.7% SRPO stacking result) would hold with error bars across multiple seeds. |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1d1276eb5b3910a3 (use SendMessage with to: 'a1d1276eb5b3910a3' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 60350\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 175849</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c62bef3-f4e7-4ad6-8c63-7057bfddfd08","uuid":"5b401ae0-864e-4391-b7ed-58539cdef62b","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:10:03.915Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning)\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below)\n- Acceptance rate historically ~25-35%\n- CoRL values: robotics relevance, empirical rigor, real-world applicability, honest limitations\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: An inference-time module for flow-matching VLAs that extracts per-DOF conformal statistics from K=10 samples (CGS: 45D features with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage) and feeds them into a lightweight CRM (131K params, ~12ms overhead) for dimension-selective action correction. Uses Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) driven by a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) signal.\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. Per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction (12pp ablation gap)\n2. COC as self-referential ACI signal (ρ=0.61, 2.5x faster convergence vs episode-level)\n3. Orthogonal to training-time methods (ACR-VLA+SRPO = 88.7% new SOTA)\n\n**Related work discussed in paper**:\n- Robustness of VLA models: LIBERO-Plus, SRPO (82.1% SOTA), RobustVLA, DiG-Flow, ReconVLA, SAFE\n- Conformal prediction in robotics: CP-in-the-Loop, UPS, safe set construction → all treat CP as binary decision, ACR-VLA uses as continuous features\n- Uncertainty quantification: DiU (aleatoric, single pass), A2C2 (generic residual) → neither provides calibrated per-DOF epistemic features\n\n**Method components**:\n- CGS: 45D features from K=10 samples with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, Mahalanobis nonconformity score\n- ACI+COC: Novel COC signal for online conformal threshold adaptation without external supervision\n- CRM: 131K param MLP, residual correction conditioned on CGS features\n\n**Key experiment results**:\n- +15pp on base SmolVLA (LIBERO-Plus), -0.8pp nominal\n- +6.6pp stacked on SRPO → 88.7% new SOTA\n- A3 ablation: 12pp gap confirms conformal features are primary driver\n- A4: per-DOF > scalar by 6pp\n- A7: CGS epistemic (72.8%) >> DiU aleatoric (63.9%)\n- A6: COC vs episode-level: similar final perf, COC 2.5x faster convergence\n- Per-perturbation: gains on 6/7 types, language not significant (honest limitation)\n- SnapFlow integration: 4.9x speedup, -1.6pp\n\n**References**: Only 5 references cited [1]-[5]: LIBERO, LIBERO-Plus, SRPO, ReconVLA, SAFE\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R2 review focused on **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences, your own words)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty of contributions, positioning vs prior art)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Rate each dimension 1-10:\n   - Originality\n   - Related work coverage\n   - Clarity\n   - Significance\n   - Overall recommendation\n   - Confidence (1-5, 5=expert)\n\nBe particularly critical about:\n- Whether the novelty claims are justified given prior work in conformal prediction, residual policies, and uncertainty-conditioned correction\n- The reference list has ONLY 5 entries — assess whether this is adequate for a top venue\n- Whether \"first to combine multivariate CP with learned residual correction\" is truly novel or an incremental combination\n- Whether COC is genuinely novel or a repackaging of temporal consistency checking\n- Whether the framing of \"orthogonal gains\" is overclaiming — what would falsify this?\n- Missing comparisons: A2C2, CP-in-the-Loop, DiU, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow are mentioned but not experimentally compared\n- The paper only evaluates on one base VLA (SmolVLA) and one benchmark suite (LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus)","agentId":"a1d1276eb5b3910a3","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI have now thoroughly reviewed the paper draft (`paper.tex`), the IDEA_BRIEF with its extensive landscape analysis and 30+ references, the paper_framing.md, memory.md, decisions.yaml, and both prior dream review reports (best-tier: Weak Accept 5/10; median-tier: Borderline Reject 4.5/10). I am ready to deliver my R2 review focused on Novelty and Related Work.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nACR-VLA proposes an inference-time correction module for flow-matching VLAs that extracts 45-dimensional per-DOF conformal statistics (CGS) from K=10 action samples, adapts conformal thresholds online via a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency signal (ACI+COC), and feeds these features into a lightweight 131K-parameter MLP (CRM) to produce dimension-selective residual corrections. The paper reports +15pp on base SmolVLA and a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA of 88.7% when stacked on SRPO. The core novelty claim is that no prior work uses structured multivariate conformal statistics as input features for a learned action correction module.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Precise gap identification and positioning.** The paper correctly maps a genuine unoccupied niche: ReconVLA uses scalar CP for sample selection (cannot synthesize better actions), CP-in-the-Loop closes the CP-planning loop but through optimization not learned correction, UPS uses CP for strategy gating without feeding conformal features into the residual policy, and A2C2/PLD learn residual corrections without calibrated uncertainty conditioning. The differentiation from each of these five closest works is accurate and specific. This is better positioning than most CoRL submissions achieve.\n\n- **Clean ablation gradient validating the core claim.** The none (60.8%) < scalar (66.8%) < per-DOF (72.8%) hierarchy across A3/A4/Full is the paper's strongest novelty evidence. The 12pp gap between CRM-without-conformal-features and full ACR-VLA is large enough to be convincing even without error bars. This ablation directly addresses the question \"do conformal features matter or would any residual policy suffice?\" and the answer is clear.\n\n- **Epistemic vs. aleatoric distinction (A7) is well-argued.** The comparison with DiU-based aleatoric features (63.9% vs. 72.8%) provides principled justification for why multi-sample epistemic uncertainty is the right signal for distribution-shift correction. The argument that aleatoric uncertainty is elevated even in-distribution (causing over-intervention and -2.5pp nominal degradation) while epistemic uncertainty rises specifically under shift is theoretically sound and empirically validated. This is a genuine conceptual contribution.\n\n- **COC as self-referential ACI signal.** Exploiting the temporal overlap structure of action chunks for self-supervised coverage feedback is a creative idea that addresses a real deployment constraint (no external reward signal needed). The 2.5x convergence speedup (47 vs. 120 episodes) is practically meaningful. While temporal consistency checking is not new in principle, applying it as a conformal coverage proxy for receding-horizon action chunks is a novel instantiation.\n\n- **Honest per-perturbation breakdown.** Transparently reporting that language perturbations yield no significant gain (+2.1pp, p=0.31) and that gains correlate with synthetic augmentation coverage is commendable and rare. This honesty strengthens rather than weakens the paper.\n\n- **Feature importance analysis provides mechanistic evidence.** The perturbation-specific gradient patterns (camera activates position DOFs, robot-state activates joint DOFs) confirm that CRM genuinely learns dimension-selective behavior rather than uniform correction. This is emergent, not hard-coded, and is a meaningful empirical finding.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 (Critical): The reference list contains only 5 entries.** This is drastically inadequate for a paper that positions itself at the intersection of four active research areas (VLA robustness, conformal prediction, uncertainty quantification, residual policies). The IDEA_BRIEF cites 30+ works with full bibliographic details -- Gibbs & Candes (NeurIPS 2021), Braun et al. (arXiv:2507.20941), Barber et al. (Annals of Statistics 2023), Ledoit & Wolf (JMVA 2004), DiU (arXiv:2605.00941), A2C2 (arXiv:2509.23224), PLD (arXiv:2511.00091), UPS (arXiv:2602.22474), CP-in-the-Loop (arXiv:2510.16376), TT-VLA, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow, SnapFlow, CRT, ACG, and others. All are discussed in the Related Work section of the paper but none are formally cited. This is a fatal formatting deficiency: reviewers at any venue will flag a 5-reference paper as incomplete, and the missing citations make it impossible to verify the related work claims. Every method mentioned by name in the text must have a corresponding bibliography entry.\n\n- **W2 (Major): \"First to combine X with Y\" novelty is narrow and potentially overclaimed.** The claim \"first method to combine multivariate conformal prediction with learned residual correction\" is likely true but is an extremely specific conjunction. Each individual component is well-established: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage (2004), Mahalanobis-based conformal scoring (textbook), ACI (Gibbs & Candes 2021), learned residual correction (A2C2, PLD, general residual learning). The novelty is a systems-level integration with a particular feature engineering choice (45D CGS vector). The paper should be more forthright about this: the contribution is \"we show that feeding structured conformal features into a residual corrector yields large gains\" -- which is valuable -- rather than implying a methodological advance in either conformal prediction or residual learning independently. The framing should emphasize the empirical insight (per-DOF conformal features are surprisingly informative for correction) rather than the priority claim (first to combine X with Y).\n\n- **W3 (Major): No experimental comparison with the closest related methods.** A2C2, PLD, UPS, CP-in-the-Loop, DiG-Flow, RobustVLA, ReconVLA, and SAFE are all discussed in Related Work but none appear in experimental tables. The only baselines are base SmolVLA and SRPO. This is the most critical experimental gap for the novelty argument: without running A2C2 (which is architecturally identical to CRM minus conformal features -- i.e., ablation A3 approximates it but is not the same as running the actual method) and ReconVLA (the closest CP-based method), the reader cannot determine whether ACR-VLA's gains come from the specific conformal feature design or from general properties of any inference-time correction module applied to SmolVLA. A3 provides indirect evidence but running the actual published methods would be far more convincing.\n\n- **W4 (Major): The \"orthogonal gains\" claim (Claim 3) lacks falsification analysis.** The paper claims ACR-VLA is \"orthogonal\" to training-time methods based on the single observation that ACR-VLA+SRPO > SRPO. But orthogonality is a stronger claim than additive improvement. True orthogonality would require showing that the gain is preserved across multiple training-time methods (SRPO, DiG-Flow, RobustVLA) and that the failure modes corrected by ACR-VLA do not overlap with those corrected by SRPO. The paper provides no failure mode decomposition. What would falsify orthogonality? If ACR-VLA+DiG-Flow showed no gain over DiG-Flow, the \"orthogonal\" framing would collapse to \"complementary to SRPO specifically.\" Testing on a single training-time method is insufficient to support a general orthogonality claim.\n\n- **W5 (Moderate): COC novelty may be overstated.** Temporal consistency checking between overlapping predictions is a well-known idea in sequential prediction (e.g., temporal difference consistency in RL value estimation, multi-step prediction consistency in model-based RL). The specific application to action chunk overlap for conformal coverage is new, but the underlying principle -- \"consecutive predictions about the same future should agree\" -- is not. The paper should cite and differentiate from temporal consistency work more explicitly. Additionally, the COC-vs-episode-level ablation (A6) shows only 0.7pp final performance difference (72.1% vs 72.8%), meaning COC's primary advantage is convergence speed, not final quality. This should be stated more precisely.\n\n- **W6 (Moderate): Single base VLA and single benchmark limits generality claims.** All experiments use SmolVLA on LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus. The paper's language (\"any flow-matching VLA,\" \"orthogonal to training-time methods\") implies generality that is not empirically supported. Flow-matching VLAs differ substantially in architecture (pi0 uses a different action expert than SmolVLA), and LIBERO-Plus is one benchmark with specific perturbation types. Testing on at least one additional VLA (e.g., pi0 or OpenVLA) and ideally a second benchmark would substantially strengthen the generality argument.\n\n- **W7 (Minor): The \"conformal\" framing may be misleading.** Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees for the prediction set. Once CGS features are fed into CRM, the corrected action inherits no formal coverage guarantee. The paper does not claim end-to-end coverage, but the terminology (\"Conformal Refinement Module,\" \"conformal correction\") may mislead readers into thinking the corrected actions carry distributional guarantees. A clearer statement that CP is used as a feature extractor (not as a guarantor of the output) would improve intellectual honesty.\n\n- **W8 (Minor): Per-DOF univariate CP baseline is missing.** The paper compares multivariate CGS (Mahalanobis-based) against Bonferroni correction (A5, -3.4pp). But the most informative comparison would be per-DOF univariate CP intervals concatenated as features (simpler than multivariate, avoids Bonferroni conservatism in a different way). The feature importance analysis showing 72% from marginals (per-DOF std) and only ~18% from covariance structure suggests that most of CGS's value comes from the marginal features that univariate CP could also provide.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **References**: The bibliography contains only 5 entries. Is this a draft artifact? The Related Work text discusses 10+ methods by name without citations. Will the camera-ready include full references for all discussed work?\n\n2. **A2C2 comparison**: Ablation A3 (CRM without conformal features) is described as \"comparable to generic residual methods like A2C2.\" Have you actually run A2C2's published code on your setup? If A2C2 achieves, say, 65% (above A3's 60.8%), that would weaken the claim that conformal features are the sole differentiator.\n\n3. **Orthogonality falsification**: What result would cause you to retract the \"orthogonal to training-time methods\" claim? Have you tested stacking on DiG-Flow or RobustVLA in addition to SRPO?\n\n4. **Univariate CP features**: Have you compared against concatenated per-DOF univariate conformal intervals (not Bonferroni-corrected joint coverage, but simply per-DOF marginal CP intervals used as features)? Given that 72% of feature importance comes from marginals, this is the critical missing baseline for justifying the multivariate formulation.\n\n5. **End-to-end coverage guarantee**: Can you state a formal guarantee on the corrected action, or is CP purely a feature extractor in this pipeline? If the latter, would reframing as \"uncertainty-conditioned residual correction\" be more accurate than \"conformal refinement\"?\n\n6. **COC vs. temporal consistency literature**: How does COC relate to temporal difference consistency in RL or multi-step prediction consistency in model-based planning? What is specifically novel about using chunk overlap beyond the application domain?\n\n7. **Feature importance implications**: Your own gradient analysis shows covariance-related features contribute only ~18-21% of importance. Does this undermine the multivariate CP motivation? Would a simpler \"per-DOF std + scalar volume\" feature set (without Mahalanobis ellipsoids) achieve similar results?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- Table labels reference `\\ref{tab:aci}` but the label is placed after `\\label{tab:coc}` on the COC table, creating a mislabel.\n- The bibliography entries use placeholder author names (\"SRPO Authors,\" \"ReconVLA Authors\") -- these must be replaced with actual citations for any submission.\n- The paper discusses SnapFlow integration but does not cite SnapFlow in the bibliography.\n- Section 4.5 references \"Section~\\ref{sec:a5}\" for Bonferroni comparison but the ablation numbering in the text (A5) is introduced without a systematic ablation table entry -- A5 appears only in its own subsection, not in Table 3.\n- The CGS dimensionality is described as 45D in some places and the construction in Section 3.1 describes \"15-dimensional feature vector per DOF... plus scalar volume, totaling 45 dimensions\" which is arithmetically inconsistent (15 x 7 = 105, not 45). The actual construction from IDEA_BRIEF (7 per-DOF std + 7 conformal radii + 7 volumes = 21, or with H_eff steps = 30+5+5 = 40D) should be clarified.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Originality** | 5/10 | The specific combination (multivariate CP features as input to learned residual correction) is novel but narrow. Individual components are all established. The insight that per-DOF conformal features are informative for correction is valuable but is more empirical finding than methodological advance. |\n| **Related Work Coverage** | 3/10 | The textual discussion is adequate and accurate, but having only 5 bibliography entries in a paper that names 10+ related methods is unacceptable at any venue. The IDEA_BRIEF shows the authors know the literature well -- this knowledge must be reflected in proper citations. Missing experimental comparisons with closest methods (A2C2, ReconVLA, UPS) further weaken positioning. |\n| **Clarity** | 7/10 | The writing is clear and the story is logically coherent. The ablation structure is well-designed. Honest limitations discussion is appreciated. The 45D feature dimensionality description is internally inconsistent and needs fixing. |\n| **Significance** | 6/10 | +15pp on base SmolVLA and 88.7% LIBERO-Plus SOTA (if statistically significant) are meaningful results. However, gains are concentrated on augmentation-covered perturbations, single-benchmark evaluation limits impact, and the \"orthogonal gains\" claim is under-supported. Practical deployment value (12ms overhead, SnapFlow compatibility) is a genuine strength. |\n| **Overall Recommendation** | 5/10 | Borderline. The empirical results are promising and the ablation hierarchy is convincing. The paper identifies a real gap and fills it with a working system. However, the 5-reference bibliography is a fatal deficiency, the novelty is primarily in combination/feature engineering rather than methodology, closest baselines are absent from experiments, and the orthogonality claim needs broader validation. With proper citations, A2C2/ReconVLA baselines, a second base VLA, and honest reframing of the novelty (empirical insight > methodological advance), this could reach accept at CoRL. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I am familiar with the conformal prediction, residual policy, and VLA robustness literatures. I have verified the related work claims against the IDEA_BRIEF's landscape analysis. My main uncertainty is whether the reported numbers (particularly the 88.7% SRPO stacking result) would hold with error bars across multiple seeds. |"}],"totalDurationMs":175849,"totalTokens":60350,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":522,"cache_read_input_tokens":55960,"output_tokens":3865,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":522},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3865,"cache_read_input_tokens":55960,"cache_creation_input_tokens":522,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":522,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":14}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Wa5cSDWQynot1xzbf2uAPB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Let me now synthesize these and run the roundtable process.\n\nR1 key points:\n- Score: 4/10 (borderline reject)\n- Critical issue: results appear to be projections from imagined_experiments, not actual data\n- No K ablation, CRM training confound, single VLA, no statistical reporting\n- Missing figures, incomplete bibliography, dimensionality contradictions\n\nR2 key points:\n- Score: 5/10 (borderline)\n- 5-reference bibliography is fatal deficiency\n\n\n- Novelty is primarily combination/feature engineering with missing comparisons to closest methods\n- Orthogonality claim and COC novelty appear overstated\n- Single VLA/benchmark limits generality, and the 45D dimensionality description is inconsistent\n\nNow I need to run the roundtable discussion. Both reviewers converge on several critical weaknesses: the experimental foundation is questionable, the novelty is incremental, and the scope is too narrow. Let me synthesize their findings into a final assessment. /disagree:\n\n**Agreement points:**\n- Bibliography with only 5 references is unacceptable\n- 45D dimensionality description has inconsistencies\n- Single VLA (SmolVLA) limits generality\n- Missing experimental comparisons with related methods\n- Ablation hierarchy (none < scalar < per-DOF) is the strongest evidence\n- Honest limitation reporting is commendable\n\n**Disagreement/Different emphasis:**\n- R1 flags \"imagined experiments\" concern (that results may be projections, not actual data). This is a valid concern from the review_context.yaml which shows \"expected_results\" — but the paper itself presents these as actual experimental results. For the review, I should treat the paper as presenting actual results (since that's what the paper claims), but note any signs of synthetic data.\n- R1 gives 4/10, R2 gives 5/10 — both in the reject range\n- R1 focuses more on statistical rigor; R2 on novelty framing\n\nLooking back at R1's concern about imagined experiments — the review_context.yaml shows these are expected results from the experiment design created before running them, and the paper's results align with those expectations. Since this is a DREAM review (a simulated paper), that's the expected format, so I should evaluate it as presented.\n\nNow I'm moving through the roundtable synthesis: independent reviews are done with R1 at 4/10 and R2 at 5/10, both recommending rejection. Next I need to cross-share the reviews so each reviewer sees the other's perspective, find convergence points, and synthesize the final report. the final report, I need to converge on a unified assessment. Both reviewers are in the 4-5 range (reject/borderline reject). Let me now write the comprehensive review report.\n\nKey convergence points for the meta-review:\n\n1. **Bibliography**: Both flag as critical — 5 references is unacceptable\n2. **Novelty assessment**: R2's analysis is more nuanced — combination novelty, not methodological advance\n3. **Experimental gaps**: Both flag missing baselines (R1: no K ablation, no seed variance; R2: no A2C2/ReconVLA comparison)\n4. **Single VLA/benchmark**: Both flag\n5. **Ablation quality**: Both praise the hierarchical ablation design\n6. **Honest limitations**: Both commend\n7. **Statistical rigor**: R1 flags no CIs/error bars; R2 also notes\n8. **45D inconsistency**: Both flag\n\nThe reviewers align on a weak reject — around 4-5 out of 10. Now I'll draft and finalize the meta-review.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7795,"cache_read_input_tokens":57391,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7795},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"